ion, integration, maintenance, separation) were established by:","options":["Frederick Herzberg","Henri Fayol","Abraham Maslow","Herbert Simon"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 1 — Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance"}},{"itemId":"b6aa820a-2b62-57de-bd46-87f506133d4e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"841bd5e7-c509-40e2-a1a4-5954a544c404","sourceKey":"quiz:204","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Workforce planning, productivity enhancement, cost management, and talent acquisition fall under which HRM objective?","options":["Personal Objective","Organisational Objective","Functional Objective","Societal Objective"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 1 — Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance"}},{"itemId":"c3a76c38-c81e-5cae-8430-5c52cc442be8","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"42030323-a00a-4c81-884c-1f25f038dd8e","sourceKey":"quiz:205","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Career development, job satisfaction, work-life balance, and employee wellbeing are examples of:","options":["Functional Objectives","Societal Objectives","Financial Objectives","Personal Objectives"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 1 — Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance"}},{"itemId":"2d3415cd-f6c1-5c89-b133-b2a0f190599f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3915b695-0f1d-4f1a-a852-e7de2d794fd9","sourceKey":"quiz:206","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Gary Dessler's (2000) definition of HRM emphasizes:","options":["The lens of managerial responsibility and specific functional practices such as recruiting, screening, training, and appraisal","Shareholder profit maximisation only","The elimination of employee benefits","A purely legal perspective"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 1 — Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance"}},{"itemId":"87733475-5c51-5229-9a99-a850e20d0cc2","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e652626f-8ecb-4d43-b36e-dd0ac998c3d1","sourceKey":"quiz:207","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Wendell French's (1974) definition of HRM emphasizes:","options":["The recruitment, selection, development, utilization of, and accommodation to human resources by the organisation","Only legal compliance","Only employee termination","Only compensation and payroll"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 1 — Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance"}},{"itemId":"7c795bc7-f5e9-57e7-88d8-41e70777a00e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d42728bd-b80a-4232-9cf8-0358b374dfdf","sourceKey":"quiz:208","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A more contemporary HRM perspective described in the notes signals a shift from viewing employees as a cost to be managed toward:","options":["Ignoring employee wellbeing","Reducing all employee benefits","Eliminating the HR department entirely","Organisations adapting to the needs, expectations, and limitations of their people"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 1 — Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance"}},{"itemId":"4de1919b-6ef7-5ae6-b45f-94b021e98e1f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"389d539e-f653-4341-88f0-8c1d9e8682e5","sourceKey":"quiz:209","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of these falls under the 'Functional Objectives' of HRM?","options":["Career development","Corporate social responsibility","HR systems quality, HR metrics and analysis, compliance and governance","Work-life balance"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 1 — Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance"}},{"itemId":"3630bd89-b5b8-5528-8fa3-9d3cee91a2ec","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2e57cf08-b685-4b6e-89dd-cfc7858cec2f","sourceKey":"quiz:21","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The scope of Human Resource Management, as reflected in HRM definitions from 1960 to date, is most accurately described as encompassing:","options":["the systematic alignment of individual and organisational goals through people-centred practices","financial planning tied to workforce budgeting","exclusively recruitment and staffing activities within a firm","compliance-driven documentation of employee records"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 1 — Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance"}},{"itemId":"fc9e9e57-5cd7-527c-b2bb-fdeb65b880d5","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"582fcb41-0f0a-414e-a3be-3d5216b3cb71","sourceKey":"quiz:210","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"'Knowledge retention' as an HRM concern primarily involves:","options":["Preserving valuable organisational knowledge and expertise within the organisation","Reducing training budgets","Ignoring succession planning","Discarding institutional knowledge when employees leave"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 1 — Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance"}},{"itemId":"662f8caf-1f8e-5704-9945-16f94d13684c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"53d93ca0-8cbb-4640-ad7a-549637b03d6a","sourceKey":"quiz:211","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of these is one of the operational dimensions of Personnel Management identified through classical management functions established by Henri Fayol?","options":["Procurement","Advertising","Currency exchange","Manufacturing"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 1 — Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance"}},{"itemId":"6eba2b95-436d-5b38-8ba6-bbe9b9014346","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8c257b71-6fe6-4171-bd0c-8a7c38787b5e","sourceKey":"quiz:212","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"HRM's role in 'maintaining employee relations' primarily involves:","options":["Managing the relationship between the organisation and its workforce to sustain a productive work environment","Outsourcing all communication with staff","Only handling employee complaints once a year","Avoiding all contact with employees"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 1 — Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance"}},{"itemId":"ae3701e2-c7b9-5664-b3ea-4393d477cd2d","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7448c4f7-8d0b-49ef-b5b5-c07b187073e0","sourceKey":"quiz:213","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"'Talent acquisition', listed under organisational objectives of HRM, refers to:","options":["Only training existing staff","Firing underperforming staff","Reducing the size of the HR department","The process of identifying and recruiting skilled individuals to meet organisational needs"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 1 — Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance"}},{"itemId":"62488534-1732-5709-beed-24ef136fcf17","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4a258421-0332-4c0a-940c-8c185198228e","sourceKey":"quiz:214","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Michael Armstrong (1994) described human capital as:","options":["The organisation's most valued asset, not a liability","An unnecessary expense","A liability on the business","Irrelevant to organisational strategy"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 1 — Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance"}},{"itemId":"311fbc53-7945-5c0e-be4c-03c84c1c2fc3","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"efd6e9d5-daa3-400d-b56f-439ea83169d7","sourceKey":"quiz:215","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT listed as a factor that necessitates organisational change?","options":["Increased competition","Technological changes and innovation","A fixed and unchanging economy","Changes in customer expectations"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 4 — Organisational Change and Change Management"}},{"itemId":"bda41acf-4488-5362-961d-cb136b781756","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6a955bd6-93cd-4b7a-aa7e-495a15748031","sourceKey":"quiz:216","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Organisational 'structure' in the context of change refers to:","options":["The physical building where the company operates","The way an organisation is arranged, including departments, reporting lines, and responsibilities","The list of company shareholders","The company's marketing strategy"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 4 — Organisational Change and Change Management"}},{"itemId":"0b3347c0-6569-545e-9016-8c2d4d25e434","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"701e4e51-b1c7-4f9b-bade-8557563c9f17","sourceKey":"quiz:217","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Change management involving training and development programs to help employees acquire new skills relates to:","options":["Changes in structure","Changes in systems","Changes in employees' skills","Changes in strategy"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 4 — Organisational Change and Change Management"}},{"itemId":"d4a21243-c8d1-5351-8d20-064bc3e81212","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3c2428c0-cadc-41bf-9515-c74cd850660c","sourceKey":"quiz:218","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"According to Fields (2008), how many types/levels of organisational change are identified?","options":["Two","Four","Three","Five"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 4 — Organisational Change and Change Management"}},{"itemId":"44b8f166-a817-5109-a2f2-5b27bd4cdad4","courseId":"