conflict","Increases employee motivation"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 6 — Organisational Behaviour, Communication, and Team Dynamics"}},{"itemId":"492d9864-2a4d-59e4-8b9a-ea35dc0f5c6e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"adfd805f-3b65-41d2-b5f0-b2c23cff07d1","sourceKey":"quiz:262","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In the communication process, 'encoding' refers to:","options":["The medium through which a message travels","The response given by the receiver","Interpreting a received message","The process of converting ideas into words, symbols, images, or other forms of communication"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 8 — Business Communication, Information Systems, and Digital Management"}},{"itemId":"dd625f99-3b1e-529d-ae79-1b5af53c9a5e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8b9b0ce8-3244-45bb-b3bb-e3be4a1ebc27","sourceKey":"quiz:263","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The person or organisation that initiates a message is known as the:","options":["Receiver","Sender","Channel","Decoder"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 8 — Business Communication, Information Systems, and Digital Management"}},{"itemId":"21394906-780c-5de4-917d-5bc239f7a3ce","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"499d246d-5180-4408-9f55-caf905c9379d","sourceKey":"quiz:264","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which characteristic of effective communication requires that a message be factually correct?","options":["Clarity","Accuracy","Conciseness","Timeliness"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 8 — Business Communication, Information Systems, and Digital Management"}},{"itemId":"d377e704-1ba1-55ad-9dd7-d32de7c50ad2","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"140d37ef-14a0-4ed1-8862-eb06bf5a04d7","sourceKey":"quiz:265","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A short message that omits essential information fails to be:","options":["Complete","Concise","Timely","Appropriate"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 8 — Business Communication, Information Systems, and Digital Management"}},{"itemId":"96fc4daf-3ad2-54b0-9318-3c038dcdb0af","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ce0a4fa2-7b87-4bc6-9525-4a0c51afad47","sourceKey":"quiz:266","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Formal communication channels are characterised by being:","options":["Based only on rumours","Documented and traceable to an identifiable source, following officially recognised organisational structures","Always verbal and informal","Undocumented and untraceable"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 8 — Business Communication, Information Systems, and Digital Management"}},{"itemId":"0b5fa2be-b340-5b95-9c34-1ee5c2141a32","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6f4c04b2-78c4-41b3-bc69-2ba7edf739c1","sourceKey":"quiz:267","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The grapevine is best described as:","options":["The unofficial, informal communication network through which information spreads within an organisation","A type of formal report","A scheduled team meeting","An official memo issued by top management"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 8 — Business Communication, Information Systems, and Digital Management"}},{"itemId":"6074c5aa-d8ef-5be3-a450-69f8ad27c7d3","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2579d4e9-2fca-4045-88bf-ec49efa407ab","sourceKey":"quiz:268","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"'Decoding', in the communication process, refers to:","options":["The initial idea before it is converted to words","The medium used to send a message","The process by which the receiver interprets the message","Noise interfering with the message"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 8 — Business Communication, Information Systems, and Digital Management"}},{"itemId":"f2a9abf4-f2b7-5e16-b2ac-32d35c6bfb67","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"af5a25c4-d8c1-411e-b43b-509b5a109885","sourceKey":"quiz:269","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"'Feedback', in the communication process, is best described as:","options":["The channel used to transmit a message","Interference in the communication process","The person who initiates communication","The response provided by the receiver"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 8 — Business Communication, Information Systems, and Digital Management"}},{"itemId":"5905a9fc-9a5f-5d1e-beaa-5d9cbfbeb90a","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9fd2c65f-8fc1-4803-8ff8-dd0fe3f86822","sourceKey":"quiz:27","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which pairing of author and year correctly matches an HRM definition covered in the course notes?","options":["Armstrong — 1960","Dessler — 1997","Cushway and Wood — 1986","Flippo — 1984"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 1 — Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance"}},{"itemId":"6abb453d-1304-5f6a-8bb3-fc48e09319ad","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"693737e1-bf11-461f-a823-43e1992d1b16","sourceKey":"quiz:270","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"For communication to be 'timely', information must be:","options":["Repeated multiple times daily","Delivered whenever convenient, regardless of relevance","Communicated while it is still useful","Withheld until a formal meeting"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 8 — Business Communication, Information Systems, and Digital Management"}},{"itemId":"131eb1b4-02d7-5c1a-9ecf-3ebad276bc92","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"840d4749-f23a-44e7-a3bd-e641d1495a1e","sourceKey":"quiz:271","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Formal communication channels are distinguished from informal ones mainly because they are:","options":["Based on rumours","Documented and traceable to an identifiable source, following recognised organisational structures","Never used in large organisations","Always verbal"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 8 — Business Communication, Information Systems, and Digital Management"}},{"itemId":"d78cde5d-0f10-5c6e-810c-946e17fcb405","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e91fd561-ec15-4d1a-9037-185591b894ef","sourceKey":"quiz:272","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When an employee reports an operational problem to their manager, this is an example of:","options":["Grapevine communication","Horizontal communication","Downward communication","Upward communication"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 8 — Business Communication, Information Systems, and Digital Management"}},{"itemId":"939407f0-f04d-5bc7-a236-be4bc4999892","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"47327e17-96bc-472e-96cd-ae670f289223","sourceKey":"quiz:273","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Departments sharing information with each other at a similar organisational level illustrates:","options":["Horizontal communication","External communication","Upward communication","Downward communication"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 8 — Business Communication, Information Systems, and Digital Management"}},{"itemId":"dd173ce3-3493-549a-9809-482fd6194feb","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ece0a663-1e82-4925-8702-e60a7a3696f1","sourceKey":"quiz:274","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A key disadvantage of the grapevine (informal communication) is that it:","options":["Always improves employee trust","Is always slower than formal communication","Is illegal in most organisations","May distort information, create anxiety, and damage reputations"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 8 — Business Communication, Information Systems, and Digital Management"}},{"itemId":"e306e931-c2f6-5fe2-ba54-64d859af8a04","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5479538b-65d5-4dea-a91c-8cb0eae2d7ae","sourceKey":"quiz:275","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A short message that omits essential information fails to meet which characteristic of effective communication?","options":["Completeness","Timeliness","Appropriateness","Conciseness"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 8 — Business Communication, Information Systems, and Digital Management"}},{"itemId":"1cf1c5cb-ae17-568a-9e52-0f8750bf598a","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4a609d3b-01e1-4b1d-a5b0-790c0b350248","sourceKey":"quiz:276","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of these best describes the role of 'noise' in the communication process?","options":["It is the same as feedback","It always improves message clarity","It only occurs in written communication","It is anything that interferes with accurate communication"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 8 — Business Communication, Information Systems, and Digital Management"}},{"itemId":"f0a7d75a-24dc-527c-8377-9fda90389fdb","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"864626ae-0234-4b3a-823f-0229e12bc7e8","sourceKey":"quiz:277","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT one of the functions of a financial system?","options":["Price discovery","Mobilization of savings","Risk management","Manufacturing of goods"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 10 — Nigeria's Banking, Financial System, and Securities Market"}},{"itemId":"4af4f7da-ada0-5bbf-9387-9d19d01077db","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"774df25d-2824-40be-a533-a124bd1fd914","sourceKey":"quiz:278","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In a financial market, the money market deals with:","options":["Foreign embassies","Short-term funds","Long-term funds","Real estate only"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 10 — Nigeria's Banking, Financial System, and Securities Market"}},{"itemId":"f76feb4d-e1dd-5a2e-84e3-60d3028a60fb","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"02e7d43a-22fa-4283-afeb-57d39e3126ce","sourceKey":"quiz:279","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The Nigeria Deposit Insurance Corporation (NDIC) primarily functions to:","options":["Issue government bonds","Manage pension contributions","Insure deposits and protect depositors against bank failure","Regulate the insurance industry"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 10 — Nigeria's Banking, Financial System, and Securities Market"}},{"itemId":"447b8aea-22fd-545f-a7f1-fb39bd68d7b0","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"119a31a0-654c-406c-a45d-ad5414db4e51","sourceKey":"quiz:28","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The HRM feature that emphasises promoting long-term development of the workforce most directly supports which organisational outcome?","options":["sustained employee capability and organisational growth","reduction in HR department staffing","elimination of employee turnover entirely","immediate cost reduction"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 1 — Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance"}},{"itemId":"2258c7ad-c8a9-5f8b-9b3a-2baecf0afc73","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c2768fbd-b2ab-4c2b-84ad-7d7004dfe1b1","sourceKey":"quiz:280","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Merchant banks in Nigeria are primarily focused on:","options":["Wholesale banking, investment banking, and medium-to-long term financing","Retail banking for individual savers","Issuing national currency","Regulating insurance companies"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 10 — Nigeria's Banking, Financial System, and Securities Market"}},{"itemId":"00dc2838-1270-5ddb-9814-b1de9efca078","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2349d368-8e38-4f54-83f7-7286e5b0c704","sourceKey":"quiz:281","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Financial intermediation refers to the process where financial institutions:","options":["Set exchange rates for all countries","Act as middlemen between those with surplus capital and those who need capital","Print new currency","Audit government spending"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 10 — Nigeria's Banking, Financial System, and Securities Market"}},{"itemId":"81f64208-ec04-5010-a726-a52d43eda433","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f9c113c8-2222-493b-b681-91dabb2cddee","sourceKey":"quiz:282","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In a primary market:","options":["Companies and government issue new securities to the public for the first time","Only foreign currencies are exchanged","Already existing securities are traded among investors","Goods are auctioned to the highest bidder"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 10 — Nigeria's Banking, Financial System, and Securities Market"}},{"itemId":"9f7a539f-3648-5a1f-9904-58bb0b036dcd","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5ec5342e-7a52-48e3-b562-8e95fe767d6e","sourceKey":"quiz:283","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The capital market, as opposed to the money market, deals primarily with:","options":["Short-term funds","Government salaries","Long-term funds","Foreign currency only"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 10 — Nigeria's Banking, Financial System, and Securities Market"}},{"itemId":"d9d410c9-73c9-5ed2-ab8e-72f442d2417e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2eb6db94-32c0-4a93-b16f-61ebf1959bb5","sourceKey":"quiz:284","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The Federal Mortgage Bank of Nigeria (FMBN) is best described as:","options":["A commercial bank for retail customers","The apex mortgage bank that drives housing finance","An insurance regulator","A foreign exchange bureau"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 10 — Nigeria's Banking, Financial System, and Securities Market"}},{"itemId":"8478f3ae-e18c-5898-aa7e-1d575d591765","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ad39df46-e564-4464-bd62-f4588b683ddb","sourceKey":"quiz:285","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Pension Fund Administrators (PFAs) primarily function to:","options":["Print government bonds","Issue currency","Manage and invest retirement savings","Regulate capital markets"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 10 — Nigeria's Banking, Financial System, and Securities Market"}},{"itemId":"e8b09e5f-8002-55b4-8dd9-14f0f856519b","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"aad7edd6-63a9-48c1-a892-5d18563e9e13","sourceKey":"quiz:286","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"'Maturity transformation', as a key function of financial intermediation, refers to:","options":["Converting cash into gold","Transforming a bank into an insurance company","Currency devaluation","Converting short-term deposits into long-term loans"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 10 — Nigeria's Banking, Financial System, and Securities Market"}},{"itemId":"276f103e-eedb-5f94-80c7-a2d742a40551","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"591dd25f-30ed-4e39-a41e-9cc6e486b8b8","sourceKey":"quiz:287","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In a secondary market such as the Nigerian Exchange (NGX):","options":["Companies receive direct funding from every trade","Only government bonds can be traded","New securities are issued to the public for the first time","Already-existing securities are traded, and proceeds go to the selling investor rather than the original issuer"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 10 — Nigeria's Banking, Financial System, and Securities Market"}},{"itemId":"e8aaaf63-27d6-5f56-bc77-843c2afddb99","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8a51ae82-8988-4718-8be3-edd1ce519061","sourceKey":"quiz:288","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The primary distinction between Human Resource Management (HRM) and traditional personnel administration is that HRM:","options":["Treats employees as strategic resources linked to organisational objectives","Focuses exclusively on employee welfare","Transfers all employee-related decisions to line managers","Is concerned only with recruitment and selection"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 1 — Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance","explanation":"HRM takes a strategic view of people as resources whose capabilities should support organisational objectives."}},{"itemId":"b6128495-151b-5358-b078-49da8f82e1e3","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f4c656b2-4725-448f-bb1b-6d9dfff130ef","sourceKey":"quiz:289","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following best describes human resource planning?","options":["Selecting candidates after interviews","Forecasting an organisation's future human-resource requirements and availability","Determining only the annual salary budget","Evaluating employee performance after training"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 1 — Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance","explanation":"HR planning forecasts future labour demand and supply so the organisation can have the right people at the right time."}},{"itemId":"c4ae3386-110a-5096-9cdf-c09a3911cea4","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f8138afa-4ef9-4320-a909-f6a1bd774167","sourceKey":"quiz:29","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Both HRM and Organisational Behaviour share a common concern with:","options":["understanding and managing human behaviour to improve organisational outcomes","regulating financial markets","maximising shareholder dividends","determining product pricing strategy"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 1 — Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance"}},{"itemId":"49be591e-f915-5b79-a275-d23d6c205c5b","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e581af27-f270-46a2-9a7a-2e5daad55bf8","sourceKey":"quiz:290","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The main purpose of a job description is to:","options":["Identify the best applicant for a job","Measure organisational profitability","State the duties, responsibilities and reporting relationships associated with a job","Determine an employee's annual bonus"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 1 — Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance","explanation":"A job description states what the job involves, including duties, responsibilities and reporting relationships."}},{"itemId":"2f409592-6747-53ee-8a13-1eb164c770f8","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"19bbca7e-e43f-450a-a65c-632a8b304024","sourceKey":"quiz:291","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is not normally an objective of HRM?","options":["Acquiring competent employees","Maintaining effective employee relations","Maximising employee turnover","Developing employee capabilities"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 1 — Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance","explanation":"HRM generally seeks to retain effective employees, not maximise unnecessary turnover."}},{"itemId":"98cd0646-b780-50c6-b05e-04c9e5fac3a6","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"33eb1c80-8c3d-4900-90e2-14957c25a6a0","sourceKey":"quiz:292","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A company first determines the number and type of employees required, then assesses whether existing staff can meet those requirements. This illustrates:","options":["Performance appraisal","HR planning","Employee socialisation","Job enrichment"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 1 — Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance","explanation":"HR planning compares forecast workforce requirements with workforce availability."}},{"itemId":"aef6d808-7fd0-57b6-92f8-b6ebb183f5f7","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2d5b5310-cf18-4a00-9c96-3c13fcbcca5d","sourceKey":"quiz:293","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A job specification differs from a job description because it primarily identifies:","options":["The company's financial objectives","The tasks and responsibilities of the position","The organisational mission","The qualifications, skills and characteristics required of the job holder"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 1 — Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance","explanation":"A job specification describes the human requirements of the job holder, such as skills, qualifications and attributes."}},{"itemId":"c18afcf4-b5f4-5a3b-9bdc-40ba39f0cb57","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d5670973-a96f-4ac9-8e72-30f838336502","sourceKey":"quiz:294","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following best demonstrates employee empowerment?","options":["Employees are given authority, information and resources to make appropriate decisions","Employees receive responsibilities without authority","Employees are prohibited from making operational decisions","Managers retain every decision"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 1 — Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance","explanation":"Empowerment requires appropriate authority, information and resources, not responsibility without decision rights."}},{"itemId":"4dc85423-79c1-5d3d-8165-1cdb80b90949","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1dba7b50-9c3b-404e-a139-b283ceaeb8a2","sourceKey":"quiz:295","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The process of systematically determining the tasks, responsibilities, skills and conditions associated with a job is:","options":["Promotion","Job analysis","Induction","Recruitment"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 1 — Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance","explanation":"Job analysis systematically examines job duties, responsibilities, requirements and working conditions."}},{"itemId":"e9647ef1-c3d0-5b4b-b0d3-e8582047eb3d","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5f8673d2-3903-4454-bcde-7ff6681a3eb5","sourceKey":"quiz:296","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"If an organisation has many employees approaching retirement age, HR planning should particularly focus on:","options":["Increasing job ambiguity","Succession planning and future talent requirements","Reducing all training","Eliminating recruitment permanently"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 1 — Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance","explanation":"Retirement risk makes succession planning and forecasting future talent needs especially important."}},{"itemId":"cfc91402-bde4-5dce-86b3-f710bbbc78e7","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b5297bec-edf9-4a7b-bff7-be4defda80ed","sourceKey":"quiz:297","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A company advertises a vacant position on its website and receives applications from both current employees and external candidates. The activity is primarily part of:","options":["Recruitment","Performance appraisal","Orientation","Job evaluation"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 1 — Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance","explanation":"Recruitment involves attracting a pool of potential applicants for vacancies."}},{"itemId":"bea7fcb1-58f7-508f-8c7d-c50f9c2b96f4","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1ba72b24-1e86-4c53-984c-cc6b2ea445ae","sourceKey":"quiz:298","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which recruitment method is most likely to improve the organisation's access to a wider external talent pool?","options":["External advertising","Job rotation","Promotion from within","Succession planning"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 1 — Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance","explanation":"External advertising exposes vacancies to candidates beyond the existing workforce."}},{"itemId":"ecb74344-2b41-581d-9c31-28c7bb4f1e09","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2117c7a6-6a68-4083-812a-e0945f06c4e1","sourceKey":"quiz:299","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The fundamental purpose of employee selection is to:","options":["Replace performance appraisal","Generate as many applications as possible","Identify candidates whose qualifications and characteristics best fit the job and organisation","Increase employee turnover"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 1 — Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance","explanation":"Selection evaluates applicants and identifies those most suitable for the position and organisation."}},{"itemId":"d8084626-d125-5a94-947b-f7e0d2eed5ee","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3efc99a1-ac09-42f1-8c74-287faf898f9c","sourceKey":"quiz:3","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which author’s definition of HRM solidified the \"hard vs. soft HRM\" distinction?","options":["John Storey","Gary Dessler","Wendell L. French","Bratton and Gold"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 1 — Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance","explanation":"Storey's Human Resource Management: A Critical Text (1985/1999) is credited with this distinction."}},{"itemId":"ed3b9334-0e58-5324-bc44-4a753f288605","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a8c86535-4fd4-45a0-b430-383be59e2528","sourceKey":"quiz:30","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A manager who recognises that 'no manager has formulated a single best way to manage people' while simultaneously navigating a dynamic, multifaceted business environment is best demonstrating awareness of the shared:","options":["redundancy of financial systems knowledge","rigidity of management theory","contextual and situational nature of both HRM and change management","irrelevance of organisational behaviour"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 1 — Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance"}},{"itemId":"2e58e0c6-d5d9-5903-aa39-363e9ee38d6e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e4537653-4fe3-4fd6-83b2-c6e7af0e52c8","sourceKey":"quiz:300","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is most likely to improve the validity of a selection interview?","options":["Allowing interviewer stereotypes to determine decisions","Using structured questions linked to job requirements","Selecting candidates based on appearance","Asking every applicant unrelated questions"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 1 — Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance","explanation":"Structured, job-related questions improve consistency and relevance and reduce subjective bias."}},{"itemId":"a5f55ae8-7e11-579d-87be-55764e9e75a0","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"03e408b2-30c7-440b-867b-de191a38e93f","sourceKey":"quiz:301","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which statement best distinguishes training from development?","options":["Training focuses mainly on current job requirements, while development has a broader future-oriented emphasis","Development is exclusively technical","Training is only for senior managers","Training never involves learning new skills"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 1 — Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance","explanation":"Training is commonly directed toward present job performance, while development prepares employees for broader future responsibilities."}},{"itemId":"1559d7c6-3ccb-549c-8137-6d339a21571f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"93ca533c-c803-42e5-8c4c-bcd8e89bc729","sourceKey":"quiz:302","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The main purpose of an employee orientation programme is to:","options":["Introduce new employees to the organisation, job, policies and work environment","Determine employee salaries","Determine whether employees deserve promotion","Replace recruitment"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 1 — Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance","explanation":"Orientation helps new employees understand the organisation, their roles, policies and work environment."}},{"itemId":"dc7e3907-de76-5bb9-98b7-f8c6a029c3cd","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6c69950b-c332-4a27-be26-6f830e923170","sourceKey":"quiz:303","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A company promotes an employee because of strong technical performance even though the employee has never demonstrated managerial competence. This illustrates a potential problem with:","options":["Employee orientation","Job rotation","Recruitment advertising","Promotion decisions based solely on technical performance"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 1 — Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance","explanation":"Strong technical performance does not automatically establish leadership, interpersonal or managerial competence."}},{"itemId":"e31e5f53-2fbd-53b5-8b9a-31c4e23d748f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"28bae8f1-82cf-4c3c-853c-f07d0ce5cc0b","sourceKey":"quiz:304","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A manager notices that employees are dissatisfied despite receiving competitive salaries. According to Herzberg's Two-Factor Theory, the most likely explanation is that:","options":["Salary is always a motivator","Expectancy has become negative","Hygiene factors prevent dissatisfaction but do not necessarily create satisfaction","Employees have reached self-actualisation"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 3 — Employee Motivation, Leadership, and Performance Management","explanation":"Salary is primarily a hygiene factor; improving hygiene factors can remove dissatisfaction but does not necessarily create intrinsic satisfaction."}},{"itemId":"ab6eb3ef-4944-54a2-bdfa-c58d6b0d8638","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"80bae5a4-a0f7-4418-9222-d7bf0559356f","sourceKey":"quiz:305","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Under McGregor's Theory X, managers generally assume that employees:","options":["Always prefer participative leadership","Are intrinsically motivated by challenging work","Naturally seek responsibility","Tend to avoid work and may require direction and control"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 3 — Employee Motivation, Leadership, and Performance Management","explanation":"Theory X assumes employees may dislike work, avoid responsibility and require direction or control."}},{"itemId":"f2c56854-8707-572a-92b2-31ea8c70a17f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"07af298a-5daa-459e-ae4a-186ebb37f5d6","sourceKey":"quiz:306","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is the best example of intrinsic motivation?","options":["Receiving a ₦50,000 bonus","Enjoying the satisfaction of mastering a difficult task","Receiving an additional transport allowance","Earning a promotion"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 3 — Employee Motivation, Leadership, and Performance Management","explanation":"Intrinsic motivation comes from the inherent satisfaction of the activity itself, such as mastery or achievement."}},{"itemId":"2b80a32c-b466-5b62-a22e-c11d4db792ff","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ae0b628d-6e16-471b-a9ab-34560df69341","sourceKey":"quiz:307","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"According to Maslow's hierarchy, an employee seeking recognition, status and respect is primarily seeking:","options":["Self-actualisation needs","Safety needs","Esteem needs","Physiological needs"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 3 — Employee Motivation, Leadership, and Performance Management","explanation":"Esteem needs include recognition, status, respect and a sense of achievement."}},{"itemId":"b801b21f-110c-50a1-a62c-19118d6b4f4a","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"80aa3eec-4a00-4e53-9cd0-0154665656ff","sourceKey":"quiz:308","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In Expectancy Theory, instrumentality refers to an employee's belief that:","options":["Rewards have no value","Performance will lead to a particular outcome or reward","Employees are naturally lazy","Effort will lead to performance"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 3 — Employee Motivation, Leadership, and Performance Management","explanation":"Instrumentality is the perceived link between performance and outcomes or rewards."}},{"itemId":"0569ff17-8f51-5e57-8963-6021ea28eb19","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9493c44b-cd5f-4a4e-a0c5-96980be83dd0","sourceKey":"quiz:309","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An employee believes that putting greater effort into work will improve performance, but doubts that improved performance will result in promotion. In Expectancy Theory, the major weakness is:","options":["Valence","Instrumentality","Expectancy","Equity"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 3 — Employee Motivation, Leadership, and Performance Management","explanation":"The employee believes effort leads to performance but doubts performance will produce the desired reward; that is low instrumentality."}},{"itemId":"555f9765-ec61-54fe-8ce3-89cf16c53d5e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7118f0ae-6bde-444d-b7c8-60e9afb83acf","sourceKey":"quiz:31","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"According to the notes, who is described as \"the father of motivation\"?","options":["Douglas McGregor","Abraham Maslow","Frederick Herzberg","Victor Vroom"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 3 — Employee Motivation, Leadership, and Performance Management","explanation":"The handwritten notes explicitly call Maslow the father of motivation."}},{"itemId":"24f70ddf-9136-52fe-a802-6dc03ad04fbb","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9b7182eb-0f8a-4e8a-a92b-f37cdab7e943","sourceKey":"quiz:310","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When employees receive bonuses based on measurable performance targets, the reward is primarily:","options":["Extrinsic and performance-contingent","Unrelated to motivation","Non-financial and intrinsic","Informal and non-monetary"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 3 — Employee Motivation, Leadership, and Performance Management","explanation":"A bonus is an external reward tied to performance, making it extrinsic and performance-contingent."}},{"itemId":"5cb10fed-b861-5174-959c-373d93dc14ba","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"53f340db-c0bc-47b0-9e9f-073183dc2bf8","sourceKey":"quiz:311","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In Maslow's hierarchy, self-actualisation refers primarily to:","options":["Protection from physical harm","Social acceptance","Realising one's potential and personal growth","Physical survival"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 3 — Employee Motivation, Leadership, and Performance Management","explanation":"Self-actualisation concerns fulfilling one's potential, creativity and personal growth."}},{"itemId":"a6a5d934-e646-538b-bd6b-dba3af3c45d7","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b40bdab6-14b9-43bc-9fce-1ef30a8d5466","sourceKey":"quiz:312","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A manager increases an employee's autonomy, responsibility and meaningfulness of work. According to Herzberg, this is most likely to increase:","options":["Job insecurity","Motivators","Absenteeism","Hygiene"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 3 — Employee Motivation, Leadership, and Performance Management","explanation":"Autonomy, responsibility and meaningful work are associated with Herzberg's motivating factors and job enrichment."}},{"itemId":"de54b662-b607-5c86-82fe-323dbf24b412","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d2befd19-db05-4dc3-83a0-366c83d17402","sourceKey":"quiz:313","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"McGregor's Theory Y assumes that:","options":["Employees can exercise self-direction when committed to objectives","Employees avoid responsibility under every circumstance","Employees inherently dislike work","Employees must always be coerced"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 3 — Employee Motivation, Leadership, and Performance Management","explanation":"Theory Y views employees as capable of self-direction, responsibility and commitment under appropriate conditions."}},{"itemId":"909a9028-ad7e-533b-9a01-46bcc3428908","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9f775bb2-9e2b-4b21-ba5e-3c1b0e6bebe9","sourceKey":"quiz:314","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An employee's perception that another employee receives greater rewards for similar contributions may create:","options":["Perceived inequity","Job enrichment","Self-actualisation","Organisational commitment automatically"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 3 — Employee Motivation, Leadership, and Performance Management","explanation":"Perceived imbalance between contributions and rewards can create a sense of inequity."}},{"itemId":"c06aa147-8f9d-515c-8e8c-8495da0efa77","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0cd043de-31d0-42e0-844e-7d30773da40a","sourceKey":"quiz:315","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Equity Theory suggests that employees compare:","options":["Their job description with the company logo","Their salary only with the CEO's salary","Their personality with management","Their input-output ratio with relevant comparison others"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 3 — Employee Motivation, Leadership, and Performance Management","explanation":"Equity Theory focuses on comparisons between one's perceived input-output ratio and that of relevant others."}},{"itemId":"687f9ffd-6cec-5226-bf8b-1bc36a0469c8","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5dd64397-38f6-4a25-a7d5-1398faa7f2a8","sourceKey":"quiz:316","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A reward has high valence in Expectancy Theory when:","options":["Performance is unrelated to the reward","The reward is impossible to obtain","The employee does not care about the reward","The employee strongly values the reward"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 3 — Employee Motivation, Leadership, and Performance Management","explanation":"Valence is the value or attractiveness an employee places on a particular outcome."}},{"itemId":"2142ac0d-4d40-55c7-836e-66e1be11d08e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e0cb7a91-a6a6-485c-8c3e-0d0a9a97c34d","sourceKey":"quiz:317","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which statement best describes organisational citizenship behaviour (OCB)?","options":["Formal disciplinary action","Behaviour that deliberately violates organisational rules","Mandatory attendance at work","Discretionary employee behaviour that supports organisational functioning beyond formal job requirements"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 3 — Employee Motivation, Leadership, and Performance Management","explanation":"OCB consists of discretionary helpful behaviours that support the organisation beyond formal role requirements."}},{"itemId":"71b28e16-f6b0-5679-8af7-c3f373021b41","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9cf60bc4-ec79-4fbd-a0c2-566150b8c6e5","sourceKey":"quiz:318","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A manager compares actual sales performance with predetermined targets and investigates significant deviations. This activity is most closely related to:","options":["Performance management","Job analysis","Recruitment","Socialisation"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 3 — Employee Motivation, Leadership, and Performance Management","explanation":"Performance management involves monitoring performance against expectations and using feedback to improve results."}},{"itemId":"f94c3844-f2b0-51ae-9092-3f348a3c9ed5","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"da02ff2c-0d25-41bf-b1d4-62454f8a90cc","sourceKey":"quiz:319","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A performance appraisal system is most useful when it:","options":["Is conducted only to punish poor performers","Provides feedback that can support development and performance improvement","Measures personality rather than job performance","Ignores predetermined standards"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 3 — Employee Motivation, Leadership, and Performance Management","explanation":"Effective appraisal provides evidence-based feedback that supports development and improved performance."}},{"itemId":"5216ad09-fb59-5dfa-86b7-1604f88a03a2","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8485378a-4585-4e12-a505-594e3c48f288","sourceKey":"quiz:32","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Maslow's Hierarchy of Needs, from bottom to top, follows which order?","options":["Physiological, Social, Safety, Self-Actualization, Esteem","Physiological, Safety, Social, Esteem, Self-Actualization","Safety, Physiological, Social, Esteem, Self-Actualization","Social, Physiological, Safety, Esteem, Self-Actualization"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 3 — Employee Motivation, Leadership, and Performance Management","explanation":"The correct bottom-to-top order is Physiological, Safety, Social, Esteem, Self-Actualization."}},{"itemId":"ab7f773a-f6ad-5501-a983-7d7da89650f4","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"27675d8d-f7e9-4804-ac79-7cb2a90518a4","sourceKey":"quiz:320","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An effective performance management system should primarily:","options":["Operate as a once-a-year punishment mechanism","Focus exclusively on employee weaknesses","Eliminate employee participation","Align individual performance with organisational objectives through ongoing feedback and development"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 3 — Employee Motivation, Leadership, and Performance Management","explanation":"Performance management is continuous and links individual objectives, feedback, development and organisational strategy."}},{"itemId":"6a6bcde0-7e65-5b4d-9253-5201e47d0adc","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b70daa17-2744-49d3-a0d5-e1b4d14144bc","sourceKey":"quiz:321","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A 360-degree appraisal obtains performance information from:","options":["Only external recruiters","Only the employee's family members","Multiple sources such as supervisors, peers, subordinates and sometimes customers","Only the employee's immediate supervisor"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 3 — Employee Motivation, Leadership, and Performance Management","explanation":"360-degree feedback uses multiple perspectives, often including supervisors, peers, subordinates and customers."}},{"itemId":"e8d432b4-e9aa-5292-b680-c2a4defb491c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"df3696a3-c12d-4e4c-8408-d839efb011ab","sourceKey":"quiz:322","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which appraisal error occurs when one positive characteristic causes an evaluator to rate an employee highly across unrelated dimensions?","options":["Contrast effect","Recency effect","Central tendency","Halo effect"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 3 — Employee Motivation, Leadership, and Performance Management","explanation":"The halo effect occurs when one favourable characteristic influences ratings on other dimensions."}},{"itemId":"862107c8-1910-51a1-85f3-68d57847a07f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6f390d67-14ce-4e27-8308-c45cbf3cf7f2","sourceKey":"quiz:323","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A manager rates nearly all employees as average despite significant differences in performance. This is an example of:","options":["Central tendency bias","Self-serving bias","Halo effect","Recency bias"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 3 — Employee Motivation, Leadership, and Performance Management","explanation":"Central tendency bias causes evaluators to cluster ratings around the middle rather than distinguish performance levels."}},{"itemId":"efb11823-03f6-5caa-8dea-c98765ce151a","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8eea4a6f-d5e1-41fc-bb51-c972364f2382","sourceKey":"quiz:324","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A manager who involves employees in decision-making and encourages participation is demonstrating:","options":["Laissez-faire avoidance","Democratic/participative leadership","Autocratic leadership","Coercive leadership"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 3 — Employee Motivation, Leadership, and Performance Management","explanation":"Participative leadership involves employees in decisions and encourages their contribution."}},{"itemId":"17bbb6ed-b385-59af-979a-e07290daf57b","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9473def2-9f07-4d25-ac9d-d913dce53e7d","sourceKey":"quiz:325","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which leadership style is characterised by substantial employee freedom and limited direct supervision?","options":["Bureaucratic","Democratic","Laissez-faire","Autocratic"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 3 — Employee Motivation, Leadership, and Performance Management","explanation":"Laissez-faire leadership gives employees substantial autonomy with relatively limited direct supervision."}},{"itemId":"b0413823-3356-593b-b784-c6528423dd90","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"11b435d7-654d-4e3f-9e35-60b64b03377d","sourceKey":"quiz:326","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which stage of Lewin's change model involves challenging existing assumptions and preparing employees psychologically for change?","options":["Refreezing","Unfreezing","Institutionalising","Reinforcing"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 4 — Organisational Change and Change Management","explanation":"Unfreezing prepares people to abandon established patterns and recognise the need for change."}},{"itemId":"3aa06bf0-685b-5e7e-aecd-cd7671817881","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"38a0992f-5835-4d0c-a5c5-25a0a5c36f4c","sourceKey":"quiz:327","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In Kotter's change model, creating a sense of urgency is important because it:","options":["Guarantees that change will succeed","Replaces the need for communication","Eliminates all employee resistance immediately","Creates recognition that maintaining the status quo may be undesirable"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 4 — Organisational Change and Change Management","explanation":"Urgency establishes why change is necessary and why maintaining the status quo is risky or undesirable."}},{"itemId":"b1139bc4-d854-5abc-9593-445fe57251a8","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1b91be0d-1b5e-4972-872c-a7349a6d721e","sourceKey":"quiz:328","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An organisation introduces a new accounting system. Employees resist because they fear they may not possess the skills needed to use it. The resistance is primarily caused by:","options":["Social loafing","Excessive organisational culture","Goal displacement","Fear of the unknown/inadequacy"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 4 — Organisational Change and Change Management","explanation":"Resistance can arise when employees fear unfamiliar technology or doubt their ability to cope with the new requirements."}},{"itemId":"a045fdb8-428d-5cae-a720-d0123dafeaea","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"06ca59bb-ead2-4501-a8a3-6c61d4c8707c","sourceKey":"quiz:329","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is most likely to reduce resistance to organisational change?","options":["Withholding information","Avoiding feedback","Threatening employees immediately","Involving employees and communicating the reasons for change"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 4 — Organisational Change and Change Management","explanation":"Participation and clear communication can build understanding, trust and commitment to change."}},{"itemId":"af4c18c9-1a2b-5f0f-875e-b6f70e5d2885","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"da9b26fe-a138-44dd-b734-14a5e671a4e8","sourceKey":"quiz:33","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In Herzberg's Two-Factor Theory, which pair of factors form the theory's core?","options":["Theory X and Theory Y","Job Content and Job Context","Expectancy and Instrumentality","Push and Pull factors"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 3 — Employee Motivation, Leadership, and Performance Management","explanation":"Herzberg's theory rests on Job Content and Job Context, not McGregor's or Vroom's terms."}},{"itemId":"f5aca503-fb8a-534c-902b-1fdcca55bc0b","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5d9a56fd-424d-4299-8326-968b311bc468","sourceKey":"quiz:330","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Kotter's model emphasises the creation of a guiding coalition because:","options":["One manager should make every change decision","It eliminates the need for urgency","Change often requires influential individuals working together to lead and support transformation","Employees should be excluded from change"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 4 — Organisational Change and Change Management","explanation":"A guiding coalition provides the influence, credibility and leadership needed to drive transformation."}},{"itemId":"0670d158-bc44-5887-9074-ff5fb98d6c4a","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5b85325e-c5cd-4837-849b-6304fbeb85e4","sourceKey":"quiz:331","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"During organisational change, the refreezing stage in Lewin's model seeks to:","options":["Identify the initial problem","Create awareness of the need for change","Generate resistance","Stabilise and reinforce new behaviours and practices"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 4 — Organisational Change and Change Management","explanation":"Refreezing embeds new behaviours and practices so the changed state becomes stable."}},{"itemId":"994d4aed-cc59-542d-bfca-2dc1a469f3e3","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5fdb316d-947b-4bef-a26a-c5f671371df0","sourceKey":"quiz:332","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which sequence correctly represents Lewin's three-stage change model?","options":["Refreeze → Change → Unfreeze","Change → Unfreeze → Refreeze","Unfreeze → Change/Move → Refreeze","Plan → Recruit → Refreeze"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 4 — Organisational Change and Change Management","explanation":"Lewin's model progresses from unfreezing the current state, through movement/change, to refreezing the new state."}},{"itemId":"7f7859f9-ee83-5921-9ab6-e9f93c021c2d","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e8bcaa07-c9f7-4424-9f4e-64686dfde163","sourceKey":"quiz:333","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In Kotter's model, short-term wins are important because they:","options":["Replace the guiding coalition","Make long-term change unnecessary","Eliminate the need for a vision","Demonstrate progress and help sustain support for the transformation"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 4 — Organisational Change and Change Management","explanation":"Visible early successes build credibility, demonstrate progress and help maintain momentum."}},{"itemId":"4d3ed79b-516c-56a0-9437-803487d8550e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"871af934-52d9-4932-970b-3e78554730fc","sourceKey":"quiz:334","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A company communicates a new strategic vision but fails to alter reward systems that reinforce old behaviour. This is likely to undermine change because:","options":["Employees do not need incentives","Organisational systems can reinforce the old state","Communication always causes resistance","Vision is irrelevant to change"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 4 — Organisational Change and Change Management","explanation":"Systems, structures and reward mechanisms can reinforce old behaviours unless they are aligned with the desired change."}},{"itemId":"ae4e74ee-b1b2-55f7-b846-066ed5b54313","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"702821ba-73f9-4307-831c-af2407a4a8a0","sourceKey":"quiz:335","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A manager identifies several possible solutions to a problem before selecting one. The activity represents which stage of decision-making?","options":["Evaluation of results","Implementation","Problem recognition","Generation of alternatives"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 5 — Managerial Decision-Making and Problem Solving","explanation":"Generating alternatives expands the set of possible solutions before evaluation and selection."}},{"itemId":"35c39a42-bea7-5b1a-a446-55c38bf66509","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"537a5b50-1b27-4b3a-b23e-9893cee91aa8","sourceKey":"quiz:336","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A manager faces a situation where several alternatives are available, but none provides a perfect outcome. The manager is operating under:","options":["Certainty","Risk","Complete information","Uncertainty"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 5 — Managerial Decision-Making and Problem Solving","explanation":"Where outcomes cannot be predicted with certainty, decision-making may involve uncertainty; risk specifically implies estimable probabilities."}},{"itemId":"67c4e6d0-d540-5bb6-b4c0-518d6747d096","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"aaf1fd13-bc4c-4752-b981-c4fa55540daf","sourceKey":"quiz:337","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A decision-maker uses a rule of thumb developed from experience to solve a recurring problem. This is an example of:","options":["Benchmarking","Job enrichment","Heuristic","Formalisation"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 5 — Managerial Decision-Making and Problem Solving","explanation":"A heuristic is a practical rule of thumb that simplifies decision-making."}},{"itemId":"ddbb7a03-206e-5015-a528-7968d07f9333","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4b9645b3-ea74-4da3-8597-bf9ca6d18d67","sourceKey":"quiz:338","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A problem-solving approach that first asks 'What is the root cause?' rather than treating symptoms is based on:","options":["Cause-and-effect analysis","Job enlargement","Social loafing","External recruitment"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 5 — Managerial Decision-Making and Problem Solving","explanation":"Cause-and-effect analysis seeks underlying causes rather than merely addressing visible symptoms."}},{"itemId":"af9aca34-0510-5803-9d70-29a5c5a1ef70","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1fed6f6f-e6e3-4649-979c-0afb8d2aa58a","sourceKey":"quiz:339","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The 5 Whys technique is primarily used to:","options":["Identify underlying causes of a problem by repeatedly questioning why it occurred","Measure market share","Determine employee salaries","Calculate employee turnover"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 5 — Managerial Decision-Making and Problem Solving","explanation":"The 5 Whys method repeatedly asks why a problem occurred to move from symptoms toward root causes."}},{"itemId":"e9e6009d-a5c7-5b88-b6a3-a5bc417ff85b","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6e9fef4d-9bf2-4487-bca9-f85728ef271c","sourceKey":"quiz:34","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In Herzberg's theory, which factor group includes achievement, growth, responsibility, and challenge?","options":["Hygiene factors","Job Context","Job Content","Safety factors"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 3 — Employee Motivation, Leadership, and Performance Management","explanation":"Job Content covers achievement, growth, responsibility, and challenge found in the job itself."}},{"itemId":"225b2831-b6b4-5cb4-b6c4-2f85825e0b5c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"00efff9c-af59-47ce-8430-fe82c1a71282","sourceKey":"quiz:340","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A manager generates ideas without initially judging them, then evaluates the ideas later. This method is:","options":["Benchmarking","Auditing","Brainstorming","Forecasting"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 5 — Managerial Decision-Making and Problem Solving","explanation":"Brainstorming separates idea generation from evaluation to encourage a wider range of alternatives."}},{"itemId":"58ca85f8-e2bf-5dfc-bede-52326be19b3a","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a7993a6f-993b-45fb-8bc6-3308f65ba87b","sourceKey":"quiz:341","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A decision made in a situation where the probabilities of alternative outcomes can reasonably be estimated is a decision under:","options":["Ignorance","Certainty","Risk","Complete ambiguity"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 5 — Managerial Decision-Making and Problem Solving","explanation":"Risk exists when possible outcomes are uncertain but their probabilities can reasonably be estimated."}},{"itemId":"bcee6685-f9b2-5ba1-b79f-9b33578b3a9c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3eef2de4-c4f8-4857-9fba-4c12f04ed508","sourceKey":"quiz:342","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A manager must choose between alternatives without knowing the probabilities of future outcomes. This represents decision-making under:","options":["Risk","Certainty","Uncertainty","Perfect information"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 5 — Managerial Decision-Making and Problem Solving","explanation":"Uncertainty exists when the probabilities of future outcomes cannot reasonably be assigned."}},{"itemId":"0b784216-87a6-5861-8146-945934bb8bc4","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"aa825757-53c0-4b56-89e5-f865e6c5d569","sourceKey":"quiz:343","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A team agrees to accept a poor-quality proposal because members are more concerned with maintaining harmony than critically evaluating alternatives. This is most closely associated with:","options":["Delegation","Social facilitation","Groupthink","Organisational citizenship"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 6 — Organisational Behaviour, Communication, and Team Dynamics","explanation":"Groupthink occurs when the desire for consensus suppresses critical evaluation of alternatives."}},{"itemId":"49f948af-7936-53b3-b7e4-85d8ba491097","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a98feb0f-768f-4527-80c1-c247c3c9f97b","sourceKey":"quiz:344","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In organisational behaviour, role conflict occurs when:","options":["An employee has no assigned responsibilities","Employees agree completely about their responsibilities","A manager delegates effectively","An individual faces incompatible expectations associated with different roles"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 6 — Organisational Behaviour, Communication, and Team Dynamics","explanation":"Role conflict occurs when expectations from different roles or sources are incompatible."}},{"itemId":"989262e7-b1c8-511e-a0c0-2e3ed32e7d88","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8e3b4ae4-5dba-4cd6-9d2a-cd32ec5bb7c3","sourceKey":"quiz:345","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is a team process loss?","options":["Collective learning","Social loafing","Knowledge sharing","Synergy"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 6 — Organisational Behaviour, Communication, and Team Dynamics","explanation":"Social loafing reduces group effort when individuals exert less effort collectively than they would individually."}},{"itemId":"fc73c4e7-e63a-5576-a946-34b6436098fa","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"963b43b5-be69-4075-a490-bc6e5701269d","sourceKey":"quiz:346","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The tendency for individuals to exert less effort when working collectively than when working alone is:","options":["Social loafing","Group cohesion","Socialisation","Group polarisation"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 6 — Organisational Behaviour, Communication, and Team Dynamics","explanation":"Social loafing is reduced individual effort in group settings."}},{"itemId":"0b05bd4d-0810-56d9-a870-48f4d8265f9a","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0febf889-c88b-44e6-997e-efa5b1b4d158","sourceKey":"quiz:347","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Team cohesion is generally strengthened when:","options":["Members share goals, trust one another and experience positive interaction","Communication is discouraged","Roles are completely undefined","Members have incompatible objectives"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 6 — Organisational Behaviour, Communication, and Team Dynamics","explanation":"Shared goals, trust and positive interaction strengthen attachment and cooperation within teams."}},{"itemId":"cc93659b-375b-5a8e-bdaf-a1ad7856a466","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"96999dd3-eddb-4481-b744-ee5e82458a2b","sourceKey":"quiz:348","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A team whose members possess complementary skills and work toward a shared purpose is more likely to demonstrate:","options":["Role ambiguity","Bureaucracy","Synergy","Dysfunction"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 6 — Organisational Behaviour, Communication, and Team Dynamics","explanation":"Synergy occurs when coordinated team effort produces benefits greater than the simple sum of individual contributions."}},{"itemId":"98a47084-f8cb-535d-a781-751946fd1c34","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"243644df-97c6-42b9-9e0e-219e957985d3","sourceKey":"quiz:349","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is most characteristic of an organic organisational structure?","options":["Flexible roles and decentralised decision-making","Extensive bureaucratic controls","Strict formalisation and narrow communication channels","Rigid hierarchy and centralisation"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 6 — Organisational Behaviour, Communication, and Team Dynamics","explanation":"Organic structures favour flexibility, decentralisation, collaboration and adaptable roles."}},{"itemId":"ea655c46-6145-51af-a504-2cde409b55db","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1f6c1e91-9f51-401d-aac8-b0640cd5a1c0","sourceKey":"quiz:35","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is classified under Herzberg's Job Context?","options":["Responsibility","Pay","Achievement","Growth"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 3 — Employee Motivation, Leadership, and Performance Management","explanation":"Pay, working conditions, supervision, and interpersonal relationships fall under Job Context."}},{"itemId":"3fbe33c3-8f1c-59f2-a365-cc11589c8147","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"27a40ff6-f847-42ae-909c-92d63cd8f9ef","sourceKey":"quiz:350","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The term span of control refers to:","options":["The number of departments in a company","The number of organisational objectives","The number of subordinates directly supervised by a manager","The geographical area covered by an organisation"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 6 — Organisational Behaviour, Communication, and Team Dynamics","explanation":"Span of control is the number of employees who report directly to a manager."}},{"itemId":"0e947f2c-b008-5ef2-ab5e-4e2d90bc4450","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3de4a983-1bc0-46fe-abfd-924aca7be216","sourceKey":"quiz:351","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An organisation's deeply held beliefs about 'how things are done around here' primarily represent its:","options":["Job specification","Formal structure","Performance standard","Organisational culture"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 6 — Organisational Behaviour, Communication, and Team Dynamics","explanation":"Organisational culture consists of shared values, assumptions, beliefs and norms shaping behaviour."}},{"itemId":"04b6c75a-3df8-5edc-ae60-5a4b7069599f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3cdd3821-7c40-4dfc-a0ac-2c308396a1af","sourceKey":"quiz:352","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A strong organisational culture can become problematic when it:","options":["Clarifies expected behaviours","Supports employee identity","Provides shared values","Prevents adaptation to environmental changes"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 6 — Organisational Behaviour, Communication, and Team Dynamics","explanation":"A strong culture can create rigidity if established assumptions prevent adaptation to changing conditions."}},{"itemId":"90e516e6-1ab1-52e0-b40e-e89c35dabaea","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"062cf73a-9abf-43e8-be98-067e50d491fd","sourceKey":"quiz:353","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which organisational culture is most likely to emphasise innovation, flexibility and adaptability?","options":["Rigid bureaucratic culture","Adhocracy culture","Clan culture","Hierarchy culture"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 6 — Organisational Behaviour, Communication, and Team Dynamics","explanation":"Adhocracy culture emphasises innovation, entrepreneurship, flexibility and adaptation."}},{"itemId":"a8875586-bf56-59c3-a4d4-788fe581b811","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a993dee5-6c97-436d-accf-6097193e196a","sourceKey":"quiz:354","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An organisation that emphasises formal procedures, stability, control and clearly defined authority is most consistent with:","options":["Adhocracy culture","Entrepreneurial culture","Hierarchy culture","Informal culture"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 6 — Organisational Behaviour, Communication, and Team Dynamics","explanation":"Hierarchy culture values formal procedures, stability, control and clearly defined authority."}},{"itemId":"4978d7ff-a702-57bf-bc2c-5596429e91ce","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9e5759da-e512-4d41-b752-83168281b3ce","sourceKey":"quiz:355","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following represents the best example of CSR?","options":["Investing in community education while maintaining responsible business practices","Increasing prices solely to maximise short-term profit","Ignoring environmental regulations to reduce costs","Manipulating financial statements"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 6 — Organisational Behaviour, Communication, and Team Dynamics","explanation":"CSR involves responsible conduct toward stakeholders and society, including meaningful community and environmental initiatives."}},{"itemId":"73596be6-b379-511b-aa63-fd8ec7346376","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d5fcfd39-25ed-42c6-90c4-5c5bd5c23549","sourceKey":"quiz:356","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is an example of ethical decision-making?","options":["Following competitors' unethical practices","Hiding material information from customers","Choosing the option that maximises profit regardless of harm","Considering stakeholders, consequences, fairness and applicable ethical principles"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 6 — Organisational Behaviour, Communication, and Team Dynamics","explanation":"Ethical decision-making considers moral principles, stakeholder interests, consequences, fairness and applicable rules."}},{"itemId":"db563e72-e3ef-5548-9164-f2d3ae59a15b","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6b24e259-f3e4-4c68-8d1c-691cd87af342","sourceKey":"quiz:357","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which statement best describes organisational politics?","options":["It involves attempts to influence decisions or outcomes, sometimes using informal power","All political behaviour is necessarily illegal","It occurs only in government organisations","It is always beneficial to employees"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 6 — Organisational Behaviour, Communication, and Team Dynamics","explanation":"Organisational politics involves influence tactics and the use of formal or informal power to affect outcomes."}},{"itemId":"ace08885-60e2-51c8-b865-940726f3f933","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"35d8c374-20a0-41f9-b4de-c49a7a697065","sourceKey":"quiz:358","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When an employee uses personal relationships to influence a promotion decision rather than relying solely on formal procedures, this may be considered:","options":["Job analysis","Organisational politics","Formalisation","Performance management"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 6 — Organisational Behaviour, Communication, and Team Dynamics","explanation":"Using informal influence and relationships to shape organisational outcomes is a form of organisational politics."}},{"itemId":"0bb53573-4553-594c-888a-116796b3aeae","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"00b02a28-3a35-4701-b3da-ba53ec073765","sourceKey":"quiz:359","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Business ethics is primarily concerned with:","options":["Avoiding all competition","Maximising revenue regardless of consequences","Ensuring that managers always agree","Principles and standards that guide morally appropriate business conduct"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 6 — Organisational Behaviour, Communication, and Team Dynamics","explanation":"Business ethics applies moral principles and standards to decisions and conduct in business."}},{"itemId":"244c6f60-6ddd-589d-bc08-5014e9b60c44","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"78ddd85b-d1d2-4cf4-9a85-2021cee1ed2c","sourceKey":"quiz:36","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"According to Herzberg's theory as taught, the absence of Job Context factors:","options":["Has no effect at all","Does not motivate, but leads to dissatisfaction","Motivates employees","Always leads to resignation"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 3 — Employee Motivation, Leadership, and Performance Management","explanation":"Absence of Context leads to dissatisfaction but its presence does not itself motivate."}},{"itemId":"93b56543-af5e-5e09-bcbe-a960b7956a5c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ba5cd764-6d61-4437-a1d4-701a02fd9778","sourceKey":"quiz:360","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Corporate Social Responsibility (CSR) extends the organisation's responsibility beyond shareholders to include:","options":["Only competitors","Only senior managers","Only government agencies","Relevant stakeholders and broader social/environmental impacts"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 6 — Organisational Behaviour, Communication, and Team Dynamics","explanation":"CSR recognises responsibilities to wider stakeholders and social and environmental impacts."}},{"itemId":"1c8bca9e-6c8f-5cb6-afff-752e07c1f168","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0b2f1a34-8164-4875-84c6-8fe77f0d0aa8","sourceKey":"quiz:361","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A company reduces pollution because it recognises a responsibility to the community and environment, even beyond minimum legal requirements. This illustrates:","options":["Job enrichment","Recruitment","CSR","Centralisation"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 6 — Organisational Behaviour, Communication, and Team Dynamics","explanation":"Going beyond minimum legal compliance to address social and environmental responsibilities is consistent with CSR."}},{"itemId":"d7e97271-928a-5bb4-b896-bfe06fc759c1","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b9058f71-83b5-4ce9-9ad4-ddc70cb5f87f","sourceKey":"quiz:362","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A manager discovers that a supplier has offered a personal financial benefit in exchange for awarding a contract. Accepting it would most clearly create a:","options":["Performance appraisal","Market opportunity","Job enlargement","Conflict of interest"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 6 — Organisational Behaviour, Communication, and Team Dynamics","explanation":"A personal benefit that could improperly influence a business decision creates a conflict of interest and may also constitute bribery."}},{"itemId":"c2337cae-2f73-506b-b789-f8bf9aef40cf","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6bd496d7-beaf-4266-8a5b-eb1fdb9621e4","sourceKey":"quiz:363","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The informal network through which employees exchange unofficial information is commonly known as:","options":["Grapevine communication","Vertical reporting","Downward communication","Formal communication"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 8 — Business Communication, Information Systems, and Digital Management","explanation":"The grapevine is an informal communication network through which unofficial information spreads."}},{"itemId":"07e98a9f-a84a-5a10-aef0-4140bacaed2d","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e9d80fcd-052f-4bd2-a52a-06fe1e3d9dde","sourceKey":"quiz:364","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which communication channel is generally most appropriate for conveying complex, sensitive interpersonal information?","options":["Face-to-face communication","A generic billboard","A mass advertisement","An automated public notice"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 8 — Business Communication, Information Systems, and Digital Management","explanation":"Face-to-face communication provides rich cues, immediate feedback and greater opportunity to manage sensitive issues."}},{"itemId":"85af9ff5-1fd7-5042-b24b-5c2ba143d437","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e52663d9-eba3-462f-a952-a1f860503501","sourceKey":"quiz:365","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Business communication is most effective when:","options":["The sender uses complex language regardless of the receiver","Feedback from the receiver","The communication objective","Anything that interferes with accurate transmission or interpretation of a message"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 8 — Business Communication, Information Systems, and Digital Management","explanation":"Feedback allows the sender to determine whether the message was received and understood as intended."}},{"itemId":"e3a63463-2d1a-51ed-82bf-1806e8dfe396","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4b564e65-29e5-49ff-9387-4e9f068c4411","sourceKey":"quiz:366","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is most likely to constitute a communication barrier in an organisation?","options":["Appropriate choice of communication channel","Clear feedback from the receiver","Noise, ambiguous language or information overload","A well-defined communication objective"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 8 — Business Communication, Information Systems, and Digital Management","explanation":"Communication barriers are factors such as noise, ambiguity and information overload that interfere with accurate transmission or interpretation of a message."}},{"itemId":"eaff040a-f720-58f6-ac4b-b1a3bd0e2126","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5b7c0385-f534-487a-8a0d-c629fe84d3b1","sourceKey":"quiz:367","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When a manager communicates instructions to subordinates, the direction of communication is:","options":["Upward","Downward","Diagonal only","Horizontal"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 8 — Business Communication, Information Systems, and Digital Management","explanation":"Communication from managers to subordinates is downward communication."}},{"itemId":"724d5b47-ba57-5688-9d1a-485ac946bd15","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3eb9815b-8860-465c-bb19-3e65be920f73","sourceKey":"quiz:368","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When employees provide performance feedback and concerns to management, this represents:","options":["External communication","Lateral communication only","Downward communication","Upward communication"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 8 — Business Communication, Information Systems, and Digital Management","explanation":"Upward communication flows from employees/subordinates to managers and senior decision-makers."}},{"itemId":"87f6db81-ba94-5072-82f4-02c16bd8f4a9","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8503b5a9-1201-4dbc-9d5b-fd171e0b0244","sourceKey":"quiz:369","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An information system that records routine transactions such as sales, receipts and payments is primarily a:","options":["Decision Support System","Executive Support System","Expert system","Transaction Processing System"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 8 — Business Communication, Information Systems, and Digital Management","explanation":"A TPS captures and processes routine, repetitive transactions."}},{"itemId":"043f17f9-fe4f-5719-9804-98fe2e5c700d","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"554ad80a-2afe-485f-b9fd-829e87c26783","sourceKey":"quiz:37","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The need to \"be free from danger\" and to work in a safe environment corresponds to which level of Maslow’s hierarchy?","options":["Physiological","Safety","Esteem","Self-Actualization"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 3 — Employee Motivation, Leadership, and Performance Management","explanation":"Safety needs cover freedom from danger and a safe work environment."}},{"itemId":"18b48f80-c297-5ce7-9772-70afd61707b9","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2066e175-2a3c-499d-b457-d7acf3d9e43f","sourceKey":"quiz:370","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A manager uses historical data and analytical models to evaluate alternative business decisions. This is characteristic of:","options":["Manual filing system","Decision Support System","Payroll register","Transaction Processing System"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 8 — Business Communication, Information Systems, and Digital Management","explanation":"A DSS supports analysis and evaluation of alternatives using data and analytical models."}},{"itemId":"83e05c38-fc68-531a-a685-821c1a71e16c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"23e6c917-1421-4395-ab1f-79d453ca2260","sourceKey":"quiz:371","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Digital management is primarily concerned with:","options":["Replacing strategy with technology","Restricting information access","Using digital technologies, data and platforms to improve organisational processes and decision-making","Eliminating all human employees"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 8 — Business Communication, Information Systems, and Digital Management","explanation":"Digital management applies digital technologies, data and platforms to improve processes, coordination and decisions."}},{"itemId":"0cfb13c1-6a40-57b2-ada5-846b5e75853c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a7a86df0-d3bf-41e9-9508-420ca95f6f1d","sourceKey":"quiz:372","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A major advantage of an effective HR information system (HRIS) is that it:","options":["Guarantees employee loyalty","Facilitates the collection, storage and analysis of employee information","Eliminates the need for HR professionals","Prevents all labour disputes"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 8 — Business Communication, Information Systems, and Digital Management","explanation":"HRIS improves the management and analysis of employee data; it does not replace HR judgement or guarantee outcomes."}},{"itemId":"706b5f92-b788-51af-9d72-6023b515f1aa","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9a4b05fc-29a3-47f0-ae2a-aee5f72e1887","sourceKey":"quiz:373","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An enterprise resource planning (ERP) system primarily seeks to:","options":["Prevent managers from accessing information","Eliminate accounting records","Integrate major organisational processes and information across functions","Isolate departmental data"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 8 — Business Communication, Information Systems, and Digital Management","explanation":"ERP integrates data and processes across functions such as finance, procurement, operations and HR."}},{"itemId":"bc81328f-31cb-5253-8f63-823e07bd2344","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0b79ae17-0fe8-4fec-be88-48538f104618","sourceKey":"quiz:374","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"One major strategic benefit of data analytics is that it can:","options":["Make business ethics unnecessary","Convert organisational data into insights that support evidence-based decisions","Eliminate uncertainty completely","Replace all managerial judgement"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 8 — Business Communication, Information Systems, and Digital Management","explanation":"Analytics transforms data into insights that improve evidence-based decision-making, although managerial judgement remains important."}},{"itemId":"eca96ada-5c59-50d0-b9d3-4fba713683f4","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"185c5bc5-d31a-40be-821c-ccbb295b5438","sourceKey":"quiz:375","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A company that collects customer data should be particularly concerned with:","options":["Maximising data collection regardless of purpose","Removing cybersecurity controls","Data privacy and responsible data governance","Publishing confidential information"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 8 — Business Communication, Information Systems, and Digital Management","explanation":"Responsible data governance includes privacy, appropriate use, security, access controls and compliance."}},{"itemId":"fb6fdee3-6f10-5923-92b1-ba7714ef2a19","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a1a9f289-258d-4f73-8a39-fa274286ca79","sourceKey":"quiz:376","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The Nigerian financial system can broadly be understood as a system that:","options":["Facilitates the mobilisation, allocation and transfer of financial resources","Exists only to provide bank loans","Deals exclusively with government taxation","Operates independently of financial institutions"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 10 — Nigeria's Banking, Financial System, and Securities Market","explanation":"A financial system channels funds from surplus units to deficit units and supports payments, investment and resource allocation."}},{"itemId":"5e04edaa-1435-5874-9e51-7f063d64a74f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a612fcdf-7262-46dd-8620-af5021980d97","sourceKey":"quiz:377","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which institution is the apex monetary authority in Nigeria?","options":["Corporate Affairs Commission","Central Bank of Nigeria","Securities and Exchange Commission","Nigerian Exchange Group"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 10 — Nigeria's Banking, Financial System, and Securities Market","explanation":"The Central Bank of Nigeria is the country's central monetary authority and banking-system regulator."}},{"itemId":"063cca4c-ef5e-5738-aa88-c022e7787125","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"95eb9409-f263-42a4-a306-14f95eb463fd","sourceKey":"quiz:378","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The Central Bank of Nigeria (CBN) primarily performs which function?","options":["Formulating and implementing monetary policy and regulating the banking system","Managing the Nigerian Exchange directly","Operating every private company in Nigeria","Conducting corporate recruitment"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 10 — Nigeria's Banking, Financial System, and Securities Market","explanation":"The CBN conducts monetary policy and performs major regulatory and supervisory functions in the financial system."}},{"itemId":"f227f3fb-fd32-55bb-aade-a19f02eae542","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e6521429-51b9-4654-8549-20f98dd4afbe","sourceKey":"quiz:379","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Commercial banks primarily perform financial intermediation by:","options":["Setting stock-market prices directly","Mobilising deposits and providing credit to borrowers","Converting all deposits directly into government taxes","Issuing corporate shares on behalf of every company"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 10 — Nigeria's Banking, Financial System, and Securities Market","explanation":"Banks mobilise deposits from surplus units and channel funds to borrowers through credit facilities."}},{"itemId":"113f0de2-9d14-5505-ab3e-974c5880f517","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"23038da3-69c0-49ba-b2f8-8284d4501860","sourceKey":"quiz:38","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Making full use of one's potential and becoming whatsoever one wants to become describes which Maslow need level?","options":["Love/Social","Self-Actualization","Esteem","Safety"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 3 — Employee Motivation, Leadership, and Performance Management","explanation":"Self-Actualization is the highest level, defined this way in the notes."}},{"itemId":"fd0c434b-e2ec-50de-a9ea-035ba0733d52","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3d35c029-eeec-4b2c-90da-ce53d3dd358b","sourceKey":"quiz:380","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The Nigerian capital market differs from the money market mainly because the capital market deals predominantly with:","options":["Medium- and long-term funds and securities","Short-term funds only","Daily cash transactions only","Foreign currency exclusively"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 10 — Nigeria's Banking, Financial System, and Securities Market","explanation":"Capital markets facilitate medium- and long-term financing through securities such as shares and longer-term bonds."}},{"itemId":"7147ed23-4018-5d31-914a-974c249c79dd","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"61be849b-55fd-4593-9578-b87057929762","sourceKey":"quiz:381","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is most closely associated with the money market?","options":["Treasury bills","Long-term corporate bonds exclusively","Ordinary shares","Preference shares"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 10 — Nigeria's Banking, Financial System, and Securities Market","explanation":"Treasury bills are short-term government securities and are classic money-market instruments."}},{"itemId":"8633da72-c318-598c-b981-1237ec927497","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d5016435-8431-4767-a8c6-f7a959540482","sourceKey":"quiz:382","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The Nigerian Exchange (NGX) primarily provides a platform for:","options":["Trading listed securities","Setting monetary policy","Issuing Nigerian currency","Regulating commercial banks"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 10 — Nigeria's Banking, Financial System, and Securities Market","explanation":"The NGX provides an organised market for trading listed securities."}},{"itemId":"bf08902a-cad6-5c8e-9334-72a37080a1a4","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"704f16b0-0f51-447c-ba99-aed77b7648d3","sourceKey":"quiz:383","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) in Nigeria is primarily concerned with:","options":["Managing individual bank accounts","Regulation and development of the Nigerian capital market","Setting commercial bank reserve requirements","Printing currency"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 10 — Nigeria's Banking, Financial System, and Securities Market","explanation":"The SEC is the apex regulatory institution for Nigeria's capital market."}},{"itemId":"916601c4-9602-5f81-a532-de2e49ec962f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"58eb5c00-d6a4-4977-9cb7-faab6fd02a37","sourceKey":"quiz:384","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"When a company raises long-term capital by issuing ordinary shares to investors, it is participating primarily in the:","options":["Money market","Labour market","Capital market","Foreign exchange bureau market only"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 10 — Nigeria's Banking, Financial System, and Securities Market","explanation":"Ordinary shares are long-term capital-market securities."}},{"itemId":"59e3f7d8-b6c3-5a58-93c3-50565f5f4577","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d51fcb10-34c1-41c4-8510-0ce005b2fb72","sourceKey":"quiz:385","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An investor purchases shares of a listed Nigerian company through the securities market. The investor's return may arise primarily through:","options":["Bank overdraft interest paid by the investor","Dividends and capital appreciation","Salary and pension contributions","Employee bonuses"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 10 — Nigeria's Banking, Financial System, and Securities Market","explanation":"Shareholders may earn dividends and gains from increases in the market value of their shares."}},{"itemId":"e07ef160-4473-5033-bf02-3147fb4b08e9","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"afab08d0-5e25-4159-89e4-639375fdc0f4","sourceKey":"quiz:39","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The correct authorship of Theory X and Theory Y, as flagged in the notes, belongs to:","options":["Douglas McGregor","Frederick Herzberg","Elton Mayo","Victor Vroom"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 3 — Employee Motivation, Leadership, and Performance Management","explanation":"Theory X/Y belongs to Douglas McGregor (1960); the notes flag an error conflating this with Herzberg's theory."}},{"itemId":"707da0a2-48f1-5f41-ae0d-9f6ee72a34e3","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e7c62d9e-bdfc-45b5-b300-a8450074d717","sourceKey":"quiz:4","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"According to Michael Armstrong (1994), HRM is best characterised as:","options":["Operational and transactional","Strategic, aligned with organisational strategy","Reactive to employee complaints","Purely administrative"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 1 — Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance","explanation":"Armstrong characterised HRM as strategic, integrated into broader organisational strategy."}},{"itemId":"b0bd5736-de73-5568-908d-942d9d94ff08","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"080d50d1-e2d3-4855-aae0-157cd70c6d88","sourceKey":"quiz:40","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Expectancy Theory, correctly attributed, was developed by:","options":["Frederick Herzberg","Douglas McGregor","Victor Vroom","Abraham Maslow"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 3 — Employee Motivation, Leadership, and Performance Management","explanation":"Expectancy Theory is Victor Vroom's (1964) separate theory, distinct from Herzberg's and McGregor's."}},{"itemId":"3e9b4d78-2d97-5747-8514-4ab655e506f2","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d40e07a1-07bf-47ef-b7fe-9dd4573cc6a7","sourceKey":"quiz:41","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A satisfied need, according to the notes on motivation, is best described as:","options":["Irrelevant to behaviour","A source of dissatisfaction","Still a strong motivator","No longer a motivator"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 3 — Employee Motivation, Leadership, and Performance Management","explanation":"The notes state a satisfied need is no longer a motivator."}},{"itemId":"b37adb5b-9c36-5a03-a8e5-1f8552acbee7","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"47b01c3e-0c03-40cf-97c8-bb2540c1f654","sourceKey":"quiz:42","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Motivation, as defined in the notes, is the process through which managers:","options":["Punish poor performance","Design organizational structure","Set annual salary reviews","Initiate, direct and sustain employees' behaviour toward a desired direction"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 3 — Employee Motivation, Leadership, and Performance Management","explanation":"This is the notes' direct definition of motivation."}},{"itemId":"9eaed2a1-3472-5c3f-8de9-6c9963f8696f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8ebec788-2c8e-4bc3-8be8-d6a9246bedb0","sourceKey":"quiz:43","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"According to Maslow's Hierarchy of Needs, a need that has already been satisfied:","options":["is no longer a motivator","has no relationship to employee behaviour","becomes a stronger motivator","transforms into a safety need"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 3 — Employee Motivation, Leadership, and Performance Management"}},{"itemId":"17c88014-4aad-52fb-8043-51b77dd69ee5","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f8e53f36-3fa9-4a6e-b2ae-4f993ea24237","sourceKey":"quiz:44","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An employee who seeks recognition, self-confidence, and to be appreciated by colleagues is primarily motivated at which level of Maslow's hierarchy?","options":["Esteem","Self-actualisation","Safety","Physiological"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 3 — Employee Motivation, Leadership, and Performance Management"}},{"itemId":"d4a8172d-7f31-5a4a-8269-b654af6a45d8","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c0fa1801-ad4b-48f1-84aa-efc5f67572b0","sourceKey":"quiz:45","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"According to Herzberg's Two-Factor Theory, which of the following is classified as a job context (hygiene) factor?","options":["responsibility","growth","achievement","working conditions"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 3 — Employee Motivation, Leadership, and Performance Management"}},{"itemId":"b1b91944-58d6-5a47-b35e-0400e97b6bc3","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"cd3bbba5-d838-4c47-b1e4-3fc981400df8","sourceKey":"quiz:46","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"According to Herzberg, the presence of job content factors such as achievement and responsibility primarily results in:","options":["increased absenteeism","dissatisfaction","no observable effect on employees","satisfaction and motivation"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 3 — Employee Motivation, Leadership, and Performance Management"}},{"itemId":"6769c865-89ca-5bed-aa41-a83bb4ac6fea","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1e5c6abf-2234-4c79-abdd-7e196427de97","sourceKey":"quiz:47","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of Maslow's need categories is concerned with ensuring a safe and secure working environment?","options":["self-actualisation needs","safety needs","esteem needs","physiological needs"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 3 — Employee Motivation, Leadership, and Performance Management"}},{"itemId":"033a2e08-499c-5431-9bbb-0dcf1fba3f19","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8863d10f-2dc9-4d51-bd46-939b5f2a8891","sourceKey":"quiz:48","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The need to belong to a social group and to associate freely with others corresponds to which level of Maslow's hierarchy?","options":["esteem","love/social","physiological","self-actualisation"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 3 — Employee Motivation, Leadership, and Performance Management"}},{"itemId":"d161699c-588b-5f91-aa33-1c029a952684","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"164e5b10-25ae-41c2-84a3-1a29d39dda05","sourceKey":"quiz:49","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is classified as a job content (motivator) factor under Herzberg's theory?","options":["pay","responsibility","working conditions","supervision"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 3 — Employee Motivation, Leadership, and Performance Management"}},{"itemId":"b50340db-9822-5a26-b1c7-b7d90f15ec0e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"24f36e2e-f237-48b5-8934-1ce82dafc768","sourceKey":"quiz:5","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Bratton and Gold's (2003) definition anchors HRM in which theoretical foundation?","options":["Resource-Based View (sustainable competitive advantage)","Hawthorne Effect","Bureaucratic Theory","Scientific Management"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 1 — Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance","explanation":"Their definition ties HRM to sustainable competitive advantage via the Resource-Based View."}},{"itemId":"cba37642-04da-5700-861b-4a172354babe","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"63a9b47c-b277-496f-9ae7-f5d0ce1fe596","sourceKey":"quiz:50","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"According to Herzberg, interpersonal relationships among colleagues are classified as a:","options":["motivator factor","structural element of Organisational Behaviour","hygiene (job context) factor","self-actualisation need"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 3 — Employee Motivation, Leadership, and Performance Management"}},{"itemId":"263e5ce5-2804-5e64-b898-95e6c5c98a2e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0bf5d7a3-36e7-4ab1-8233-9f764b28de85","sourceKey":"quiz:51","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A firm that experiences high employee turnover due to poor supervision and inadequate pay is most likely facing a deficiency in:","options":["Maslow's self-actualisation needs","the components of the financial system","Herzberg's motivator factors","Herzberg's hygiene factors"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 3 — Employee Motivation, Leadership, and Performance Management"}},{"itemId":"bac960fd-4519-5c0c-96fb-11c996ee6a4c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c8b6fa66-9ba8-4fb3-b960-3ecf27ee0254","sourceKey":"quiz:52","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Physiological needs, according to Maslow, must be satisfied before other needs become motivating because:","options":["they represent the basic necessities of life and take priority over higher-order needs","they only apply to unskilled workers","they are the most complex needs to satisfy","they are unrelated to employee performance"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 3 — Employee Motivation, Leadership, and Performance Management"}},{"itemId":"9e062f1f-acc6-5883-b1c3-0c8ab862ec80","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3baf1fba-b845-4fbb-900b-fcdf6a87e25b","sourceKey":"quiz:53","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Change management is defined in the notes as the application of a systematic approach using knowledge, system, process and resources to:","options":["Eliminate all forms of resistance","Increase shareholder dividends","Leverage the benefits of change toward better/more efficient performance","Reduce headcount"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 4 — Organisational Change and Change Management","explanation":"This matches the notes' definition of change management."}},{"itemId":"dc5e9e23-6000-59ad-bffa-eaf09beeb536","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"77308cf7-03b6-4279-9bac-8ae895435a2a","sourceKey":"quiz:54","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is listed as a characteristic of the business environment in the notes?","options":["Simple","Predictable","Static","Dynamic"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 4 — Organisational Change and Change Management","explanation":"Dynamic, multifaceted, and complex are the listed characteristics."}},{"itemId":"fc363ee2-23de-54b8-b577-c654238a0751","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2e5b629a-3b70-482f-80d1-53410f1f8283","sourceKey":"quiz:55","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT listed among the factors that bring about organizational change?","options":["Technological changes/innovation","Increased competition","Globalization","Stable government regulation"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 4 — Organisational Change and Change Management","explanation":"The notes list new government regulation and deregulation as a driver, not 'stable' regulation."}},{"itemId":"5aa64057-4ff5-558f-be77-28ffce01309e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9b2bef4e-d411-4329-b50e-ed0c30817431","sourceKey":"quiz:56","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The area of possible organizational change described as \"the organization’s long-term plan for achieving its goals\" is:","options":["Technology","Structure","Systems and processes","Strategy"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 4 — Organisational Change and Change Management","explanation":"Strategy is defined this way among the Areas of Possible Change."}},{"itemId":"18bd85af-e2d5-5b32-9cbf-8308be758034","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"57c4e189-3faa-4947-a26d-220ee65cab6a","sourceKey":"quiz:57","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Creating new departments, merging teams, or changing leadership roles to improve coordination falls under which area of change?","options":["Employee attitude","Employee skills","Structure","Strategy"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 4 — Organisational Change and Change Management","explanation":"Structure covers departments, reporting lines, and responsibilities."}},{"itemId":"74f2b33b-e7b7-5db4-be12-d3509840d8d6","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"36a289d1-84a2-4f31-88f8-72420c3a3bf9","sourceKey":"quiz:58","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"According to Field (2006), the level of change that is \"implemental in nature\" and seeks to maintain existing alignment is the:","options":["Second level","Third level","Fourth level","First level"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 4 — Organisational Change and Change Management","explanation":"Field's first level is implemental, maintaining the existing system alignment."}},{"itemId":"801521f0-3b31-5d69-914d-e77989c9a316","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d4263f58-c59c-4b8e-a1d7-3261f919fd69","sourceKey":"quiz:59","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Field's (2006) second level of organizational change is described as:","options":["Strategic, questioning the organization's purpose and agenda","Implemental, maintaining existing alignment","Organization-wide, involving order, belief and values","Purely operational"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 4 — Organisational Change and Change Management","explanation":"The second level is strategic in nature."}},{"itemId":"7b2cf066-ddc7-59c2-8356-02580f515855","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"cac36ec3-adbc-4e8e-92db-e17d9d1babe0","sourceKey":"quiz:6","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Mathis and Jackson (2008) define HRM primarily as:","options":["The design of formal systems in an organization to manage human talent for accomplishing organizational goals","The utilization of individuals to achieve organizational objectives","The recruitment and selection of human resources","A distinctive approach to employment management"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 1 — Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance","explanation":"Their 2008 definition centres on formal systems design for managing human talent."}},{"itemId":"45c7a24a-f211-5a15-9237-e7ddf37a50f4","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"bffb7428-1e84-4659-841b-8ead67742756","sourceKey":"quiz:60","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Field's (2006) third level of change involves:","options":["Individual employee training","Only financial restructuring","Order, belief, values, and the interrelationship of organizational subsystems","Minor procedural adjustments"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 4 — Organisational Change and Change Management","explanation":"The third level is an organization-wide effort involving order, belief, values, and subsystem interrelationships."}},{"itemId":"0e3f3d7f-60eb-5362-962b-e5ffd033819e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"aab64de2-f397-4c50-80ab-9f8abb1cc0c7","sourceKey":"quiz:61","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The two change models named (but not yet detailed) in the Week 4 notes are Kotter's Eight-Step Model and:","options":["The Rational Decision Model","Herzberg's Two-Factor Model","Maslow's Hierarchy","Lewin's Three-Step Model"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 4 — Organisational Change and Change Management","explanation":"Lewin's 3-Step Model (Unfreeze-Change-Refreeze) is the other model named."}},{"itemId":"9cc87216-4c0b-5d1d-b725-d690cf0c5eb0","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d8fae7fa-de30-439e-ad74-e4740bacc0cd","sourceKey":"quiz:62","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which area of change involves \"training and development programs\" as employees need new knowledge and abilities?","options":["Changes in employee skills","Changes in structure","Changes in systems and processes","Changes in strategy"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 4 — Organisational Change and Change Management","explanation":"Changes in employee skills require training and development as the organisation grows."}},{"itemId":"1709b711-a234-51f0-a8ff-45294d4146a4","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"00214295-ec9d-4afe-a647-bf2ae8476d79","sourceKey":"quiz:63","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"According to the notes, if an organization is not managed well through change, the likely outcome is:","options":["Increased shareholder confidence","Automatic growth","Reduced competition","Natural death or underperformance"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 4 — Organisational Change and Change Management","explanation":"The notes state poorly-managed organisations face natural death or underperformance."}},{"itemId":"66fcd497-dee0-56aa-a1d1-1b79913a76ac","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a222d343-ef64-422a-bf30-5740171d71ca","sourceKey":"quiz:64","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Adopting new tools and software to improve efficiency, productivity, and service delivery falls under which area of change?","options":["Changes in technology","Strategy","Structure","Changes in employee attitude"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 4 — Organisational Change and Change Management","explanation":"Changes in technology cover adopting new tools/software for efficiency and competitiveness."}},{"itemId":"221c1476-1ea2-567a-ae7f-24a2ce6397f1","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ec7ef764-4b1b-4114-845c-d8cc487ad288","sourceKey":"quiz:65","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following best describes \"systems and processes\" as an area of organizational change?","options":["The organization's long-term plan","The reporting hierarchy","Employee willingness to accept change","The methods and procedures used to carry out work"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 4 — Organisational Change and Change Management","explanation":"Systems and processes are the methods/procedures used to carry out work."}},{"itemId":"0b3a26d0-dba9-56e1-b32f-40249e79c072","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4f53ea22-b2fa-471d-a266-18410d347fc8","sourceKey":"quiz:66","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Change management is most accurately defined as:","options":["a purely financial exercise in cost-cutting","the systematic application of knowledge, processes, and resources to move an organisation toward improved performance","the elimination of all organisational structures","the enforcement of rigid organisational procedures regardless of external conditions"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 4 — Organisational Change and Change Management"}},{"itemId":"71b4d2f0-78ce-56fb-9ee9-5f3108e8fe1b","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9000c401-c5bd-4fce-acc5-a60f35f30176","sourceKey":"quiz:67","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Changes that are strategic in nature and question an organisation's purpose and agenda are best classified, according to Field (2006), as:","options":["operational changes","informal changes","first-level changes","second-level changes"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 4 — Organisational Change and Change Management"}},{"itemId":"00788cd7-9ce1-52f5-8e33-c2a225847aa0","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"dab3b261-771f-4d93-9ec2-b1cd5ffeef2a","sourceKey":"quiz:68","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following would most likely act as a factor bringing about organisational change?","options":["increased competition and technological innovation","stable and unchanging customer expectations","declining global interconnectedness","absence of governmental regulation"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 4 — Organisational Change and Change Management"}},{"itemId":"fdd88a4a-f12d-5c74-a799-d93309b74b8b","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b0060c40-0c77-4b73-b08a-7d658bc1a13b","sourceKey":"quiz:69","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An organisation that revises its reporting lines and departmental hierarchy in response to growth is undergoing change in which area?","options":["systems and processes","strategy","structure","employee attitude"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 4 — Organisational Change and Change Management"}},{"itemId":"09db75b3-79e5-55c9-866b-4a4275e8b121","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3d12c706-1b1e-4c2f-bee3-aea5cc882c17","sourceKey":"quiz:7","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"R. Wayne Mondy's (2010) definition of HRM emphasizes:","options":["Operational dimensions","Strategic alignment","The utilization of individuals to achieve organizational objectives","Formal systems design"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 1 — Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance","explanation":"Mondy's definition is the most concise: utilization of individuals to achieve organizational objectives."}},{"itemId":"8772e55e-61f0-56e4-9e48-53c41a72666c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"135086f9-e1b9-46b7-a667-8faad1fea9da","sourceKey":"quiz:70","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Rising customer expectations and intensifying competition are best classified, in the context of change management, as:","options":["outcomes of successful change management","external factors that bring about organisational change","internal organisational weaknesses","components of the financial system"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 4 — Organisational Change and Change Management"}},{"itemId":"72aece05-05d6-5739-8e00-b61c5b2e6b85","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8400222b-aa3b-421b-a5d3-d08491a9e77d","sourceKey":"quiz:71","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"According to Field (2006), organisation-wide efforts involving shifts in order, belief, values, and the interrelationship of subsystem groups are classified as:","options":["second-level changes","first-level changes","third-level changes","operational adjustments"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 4 — Organisational Change and Change Management"}},{"itemId":"387cf1a7-1170-5c97-b568-acd17ee4d129","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"bbed2dcf-0886-439b-bb4e-df37121f0f56","sourceKey":"quiz:72","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An organisation restructuring its systems and processes in response to new government regulations is simultaneously demonstrating:","options":["a feature of HRM","a component of the financial system","a stage of group development","an area of possible organisational change and a factor that brings about change"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 4 — Organisational Change and Change Management"}},{"itemId":"0ec23db3-69d5-5313-88ba-93f0961be7f7","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1ea04faa-a134-4a97-b972-4ba495b456b2","sourceKey":"quiz:73","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A decision, as defined in the notes, exists when all of the following are true EXCEPT:","options":["A choice must be made","A problem or opportunity is identified","More than one course of action is possible","The outcome must already be known"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 5 — Managerial Decision-Making and Problem Solving","explanation":"A decision requires a problem/opportunity, multiple options, and a choice — not a known outcome."}},{"itemId":"39c56c91-46d3-5c59-9d99-3165465f6803","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"37aaca6a-96de-4af3-9a68-7a3873745a44","sourceKey":"quiz:74","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Strategic decisions are typically made by:","options":["Top-level management","Middle-level management","External consultants","First-line managers"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 5 — Managerial Decision-Making and Problem Solving","explanation":"Strategic decisions affect the whole organization and are made at the top level."}},{"itemId":"20971753-0a42-5d3c-8305-36a665182845","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6274f8fc-370b-4f48-a0bd-84ae85d67a85","sourceKey":"quiz:75","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Decisions \"translated into clear direction and broken down into plans that departments can implement\" describe:","options":["Tactical decisions","Operational decisions","Strategic decisions","Programmed decisions"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 5 — Managerial Decision-Making and Problem Solving","explanation":"This is the notes' definition of tactical decisions, made by middle-level management."}},{"itemId":"f19cafd2-0907-5ee0-8057-d03da5b2824a","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"db8416d1-06f5-47cc-819a-6ecf2247cd86","sourceKey":"quiz:76","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Day-to-day decisions made by first-line managers are classified as:","options":["Strategic decisions","Operational decisions","Tactical decisions","Non-programmed decisions"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 5 — Managerial Decision-Making and Problem Solving","explanation":"Operational decisions are the day-to-day decisions of first-line managers."}},{"itemId":"98c634dc-5f5a-5536-85ed-eefcdb0dd563","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"412eb3b2-3eee-4d09-a9d8-3361decd3de0","sourceKey":"quiz:77","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following best defines \"risk\" as taught in the lecture?","options":["Not knowing the future outcome of some probabilities","A probability that something could happen, but you don't know if it will","A lack of information altogether","An unplanned effect of a decision"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 5 — Managerial Decision-Making and Problem Solving","explanation":"Risk means the probability of an event is known/estimable, but whether it happens is uncertain."}},{"itemId":"e57df95b-ce34-564b-af85-e5ad54a2beaf","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"60b4e27e-37ac-4b27-afa7-ae224774f4f2","sourceKey":"quiz:78","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"\"Uncertainty,\" as distinguished from risk in the lecture, exists when:","options":["Possible outcomes are difficult to identify and reliable probabilities cannot be calculated","The manager has complete information","Probabilities can be reasonably estimated","The decision is routine and repetitive"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 5 — Managerial Decision-Making and Problem Solving","explanation":"Uncertainty means outcomes are hard to identify and probabilities cannot be reliably calculated."}},{"itemId":"211d821c-a64f-5e9c-b779-17966367c2f1","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"84ffad32-85c8-444a-9602-566af369f7b2","sourceKey":"quiz:79","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In the class example of two bags of balls, Bag A (40 red, 60 black, ratio known) represents:","options":["Uncertainty","Bounded rationality","Risk","Escalation of commitment"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 5 — Managerial Decision-Making and Problem Solving","explanation":"A known ratio lets you assign a meaningful probability — this is risk."}},{"itemId":"5638a696-665a-5322-898f-c8dd4d674dd0","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"802e14c8-6a27-43c7-8935-5dca19c6e473","sourceKey":"quiz:8","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Wendell L. French's (1974) HRM definition comes from which work?","options":["A Handbook of Personnel Management Practice","The Personnel Management Process: HR Administration and Development","Human Resource Management: A Critical Text","Principles of Personnel Management"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 1 — Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance","explanation":"French's definition is drawn from The Personnel Management Process (1974)."}},{"itemId":"f8601100-60b7-5b13-afd5-1327440e96c7","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"29f3f2a1-8d5c-4f2b-a4af-dcc759f8b753","sourceKey":"quiz:80","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In the same example, Bag B (ratio undisclosed) represents:","options":["Risk","Uncertainty","Programmed decision","Satisficing"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 5 — Managerial Decision-Making and Problem Solving","explanation":"An undisclosed ratio means no meaningful probability can be assigned — this is uncertainty."}},{"itemId":"7e8e7dec-35e8-557f-a239-b55405322062","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"646ae1ee-5852-4cad-ada5-db5c012a2225","sourceKey":"quiz:81","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Decisions that are routine, repetitive, and governed by rules, policies, or standard operating procedures are called:","options":["Strategic Decisions","Programmed Decisions","Tactical Decisions","Non-Programmed Decisions"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 5 — Managerial Decision-Making and Problem Solving","explanation":"Programmed decisions are routine and repetitive, using established rules/SOPs."}},{"itemId":"f3e23eae-a5a4-578d-9326-7005586b22c4","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1bcefae1-aa44-4b6e-8564-bda3fb095fda","sourceKey":"quiz:82","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Decisions that are unique, complex, unfamiliar, and require judgement, creativity, and analysis are called:","options":["Routine Decisions","Non-Programmed Decisions","Programmed Decisions","Operational Decisions"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 5 — Managerial Decision-Making and Problem Solving","explanation":"Non-programmed decisions require judgement, creativity, and analysis."}},{"itemId":"3a6ac3fb-352b-5d31-b03c-135be20112fe","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7b07fa42-9e26-4ae1-a44a-0ef43d3962a7","sourceKey":"quiz:83","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which is the correct first step in the managerial decision-making process, as taught?","options":["Identify the problem or opportunity","Generate alternatives","Select an alternative","Evaluate outcomes"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 5 — Managerial Decision-Making and Problem Solving","explanation":"Identifying the problem or opportunity is the first of the eight process steps."}},{"itemId":"d95acb1f-073e-53e6-9417-2b85ef7d44b7","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"19f34d0f-98d3-465e-90bd-e947dd9a5983","sourceKey":"quiz:84","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"In the decision-making process, \"establishing decision criteria\" involves:","options":["Investigating the root cause","Implementing the chosen option","Confirming the outcome","Determining what factors will be used to evaluate alternatives"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 5 — Managerial Decision-Making and Problem Solving","explanation":"This is the third step: deciding what factors (cost, quality, speed, risk, ethics) will judge alternatives."}},{"itemId":"457f2857-7984-5694-be3a-de593129da51","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d8c3cd46-01d1-4110-b058-7c1ebb03ef4e","sourceKey":"quiz:85","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The rational decision-making model assumes decision-makers do all of the following EXCEPT:","options":["Possess complete information","Clearly understand the problem","Identify all alternatives","Rely primarily on intuition and emotion"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 5 — Managerial Decision-Making and Problem Solving","explanation":"The rational model is objective and fact-based, not driven by feelings or intuition."}},{"itemId":"44693a96-185c-5c8f-8724-5f856a7fed7d","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"50a6ec4e-1253-4c2a-ab1a-f77dc500e658","sourceKey":"quiz:86","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A key limitation of the rational decision-making model, as taught, is that:","options":["It ignores organizational goals","It is too creative","Managers rarely possess complete information, unlimited time, and unlimited resources","It cannot be applied to strategic decisions"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 5 — Managerial Decision-Making and Problem Solving","explanation":"In reality, managers rarely have complete information, unlimited time, or unlimited resources."}},{"itemId":"02687306-5285-5e9e-ab89-7da64c63a65a","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c5a9793a-f6cb-41e1-bbde-085127f4a4b4","sourceKey":"quiz:87","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Bounded rationality is a concept associated with:","options":["Herbert Simon","Elton Mayo","Douglas McGregor","Victor Vroom"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 5 — Managerial Decision-Making and Problem Solving","explanation":"Herbert Simon's bounded rationality recognises real limits on decision-makers."}},{"itemId":"89af06d8-b9aa-5ccd-8cb1-5404b24d0399","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"317bc0fc-4914-454c-98d2-23fb28c535b8","sourceKey":"quiz:88","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"\"Satisficing,\" as used in the lecture, refers to:","options":["Selecting the absolute best possible solution","Delaying a decision indefinitely","Selecting an option that is good enough or satisfactory","Delegating the decision to a subordinate"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 5 — Managerial Decision-Making and Problem Solving","explanation":"Satisficing means settling for a good-enough, satisfactory option rather than the theoretical optimum."}},{"itemId":"3da01e8a-870e-591f-9c98-c4fbcacbc251","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"431fff2f-35c8-4483-940c-2e8d99def5d2","sourceKey":"quiz:89","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The bias in which a manager continues investing in a failing project because resources have already been committed is:","options":["Anchoring bias","Availability bias","Confirmation bias","Escalation of commitment"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 5 — Managerial Decision-Making and Problem Solving","explanation":"Escalation of commitment is continuing to invest due to sunk costs already spent."}},{"itemId":"cec81f1c-0eab-570a-b0b8-e59eca718530","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a3a73339-b409-4c78-a59d-d6b1732420fb","sourceKey":"quiz:9","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which is NOT one of the three core elements of an organization discussed?","options":["People","Purpose","Structure","Profit"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 1 — Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance","explanation":"The three core elements taught are Purpose, Structure, and People — profit is not one of them."}},{"itemId":"58bdf711-d725-5509-bca6-4d7f22089aef","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6c6ffc31-ffaa-49a5-a5bb-dbb6c456ffc0","sourceKey":"quiz:90","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The tendency to seek information that supports an existing belief while disregarding contradicting evidence is:","options":["Anchoring bias","Escalation of commitment","Confirmation bias","Overconfidence bias"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 5 — Managerial Decision-Making and Problem Solving","explanation":"Confirmation bias is defined this way in both the notes and the transcript."}},{"itemId":"9fde2fdd-48af-539b-adcb-69fcf3ae6486","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e77c13b0-8b3e-4376-8a81-954d27932953","sourceKey":"quiz:91","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Relying too heavily on the first information received, even after better information becomes available, describes:","options":["Overconfidence bias","Confirmation bias","Anchoring bias","Availability bias"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 5 — Managerial Decision-Making and Problem Solving","explanation":"Anchoring bias is over-reliance on the first piece of information received."}},{"itemId":"92d73f89-7a40-5282-b79d-01aef42c8125","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b057e2d5-5183-4455-b407-6c44f2e435e7","sourceKey":"quiz:92","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Judging the likelihood of an event based on how easily an example comes to mind, rather than actual probability, describes:","options":["Availability bias","Confirmation bias","Escalation of commitment","Anchoring bias"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 5 — Managerial Decision-Making and Problem Solving","explanation":"Availability bias judges likelihood by ease of recall rather than true probability."}},{"itemId":"35edefcc-4d73-5ac0-a2c7-91efe9d8a2f7","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"fdcf25f9-9b2b-418a-8e0a-99c7b9d5055e","sourceKey":"quiz:93","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The lecturer's example of fearing shark attacks more than coconut falls illustrates which bias?","options":["Confirmation bias","Overconfidence bias","Availability bias","Anchoring bias"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 5 — Managerial Decision-Making and Problem Solving","explanation":"Shark scenes are more memorable/vivid, so people overestimate their likelihood — availability bias."}},{"itemId":"113f5e55-7775-57e3-b0fa-5d964410d1fa","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0c1b6b07-148f-491f-9e7a-726aa9314da8","sourceKey":"quiz:94","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following reasons was given in the lecture for why there is no perfect condition for managerial decision-making?","options":["Managers always have complete information","Stakeholders never conflict","Resources are limited and time pressure exists","Uncertainty in the environment is always low"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 5 — Managerial Decision-Making and Problem Solving","explanation":"Limited resources, time pressure, incomplete information, and conflicting stakeholders were all cited."}},{"itemId":"264fb961-a0bb-592d-846a-dfab3a1eba18","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"91215e19-7a0d-45fd-ac2f-ed4509d5974c","sourceKey":"quiz:95","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The unintended-consequences example discussed in class (rewarding an act of bravery with citizenship) led to:","options":["Improved employee morale","No measurable effect","Reduced government spending","Copycat 'hero stunts'"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 5 — Managerial Decision-Making and Problem Solving","explanation":"Rewarding one act of bravery led to copycat hero stunts — an unintended consequence."}},{"itemId":"73a39734-dc76-5c61-a47b-d471c75fafd9","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4c594f5e-6950-4132-9222-1f9de391703f","sourceKey":"quiz:96","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"According to the lecture, why can't managers learn decision-making purely by trial and error?","options":["Trial and error is illegal","It is too time-consuming","They occupy positions of authority, so the basics must be learned deliberately","It contradicts the rational model"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 5 — Managerial Decision-Making and Problem Solving","explanation":"Because managers hold positions of authority, the basics must be learned deliberately, not by trial and error."}},{"itemId":"e50531ec-c20b-5066-92fa-f27e21fdb6b3","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"440df491-1b7d-4d44-a4b3-8bfc8af4f48c","sourceKey":"quiz:97","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which management function does decision-making relate to when asking \"what corrective action is required\"?","options":["Planning","Organising","Controlling","Leading"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 5 — Managerial Decision-Making and Problem Solving","explanation":"Controlling asks what corrective action is required — one of the four management functions."}},{"itemId":"09e3f29c-2136-516c-a7ed-e0746ba31bd2","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6cb39162-015c-4c8a-9527-39de2f056a34","sourceKey":"quiz:98","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Organisational Behaviour is best described in the notes as:","options":["The systematic behaviour of an individual within an organization","A purely financial discipline","A marketing strategy","A legal compliance framework"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 6 — Organisational Behaviour, Communication, and Team Dynamics","explanation":"This is the notes' core definition of Organisational Behaviour."}},{"itemId":"feb735cd-9709-5197-8cd4-961458db9ad9","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"463ebba7-78ee-47f1-bf7b-6fbf6b0b4ce3","sourceKey":"quiz:99","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT listed among the disciplines OB draws from?","options":["Astronomy","Psychology","Anthropology","Sociology"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 6 — Organisational Behaviour, Communication, and Team Dynamics","explanation":"OB draws from Psychology, Anthropology, Political Science, Economics, Management, and Sociology — not Astronomy."}}]$expected_content$::jsonb,
  'principles-business-administration-2: cbt_question published payload and identity match source');
