 representation of an existing or proposed system in order to predict the effect of changes","A tool used only in the physical sciences"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Section F — Operational Modelling: Model Theory & Linear Programming"}},{"itemId":"4f6b5360-d7c9-5c5e-be29-153da2d29aee","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b2479653-03f1-48d1-8707-80afbabfbbc7","sourceKey":"quiz:74","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"The three top qualities of an effective model (Sharma, 2007) are:","options":["Size, Scale, Scope","Accuracy, Precision, Reliability","Validity, Usability, Value","Cost, Speed, Simplicity"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Section F — Operational Modelling: Model Theory & Linear Programming"}},{"itemId":"6953a799-3119-5672-917d-30b6cf6c33eb","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7325e764-93e9-4977-953b-8d6849592c6a","sourceKey":"quiz:75","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Models that describe and predict facts/relationships but do NOT select the best alternative are called:","options":["Descriptive models","Normative models","Iconic models","Optimizing models"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Section F — Operational Modelling: Model Theory & Linear Programming"}},{"itemId":"86930d68-6fad-5040-a8c2-2111bb34ddd5","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3773a429-1293-4d6b-8e56-e91d3bb57ffa","sourceKey":"quiz:76","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Prescriptive models that develop objective decision rules for an optimum solution are called:","options":["Descriptive models","Normative (Optimization) models","Analogue models","Simulation models"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Section F — Operational Modelling: Model Theory & Linear Programming"}},{"itemId":"7c976a7c-4d23-50c2-8edf-8212f05ac7f2","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questi