rest N210,000. What is the Times Interest Earned ratio?","options":["3.21 times","3.89 times","4.67 times","5.15 times"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Chapter 13: Financial Statement Analysis"}},{"itemId":"9d06ed14-d2f5-588c-907a-0857f048f074","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e0a75f5d-f38c-43d0-8f45-2372c8943c5e","sourceKey":"quiz:102","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"YCT LTD: PAT N410,000; 104,464 ordinary shares. What is the Earning per Share?","options":["N2.95","N3.45","N3.92","N4.38"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Chapter 13: Financial Statement Analysis"}},{"itemId":"9bd7499a-f6c6-5f7f-b5ca-5931420b3672","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"91207f06-e9d6-4401-8cff-5639100184ca","sourceKey":"quiz:103","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"YCT LTD: Dividends N90,000; PAT N410,000. What is the Pay-out Ratio?","options":["15.80%","18.60%","21.95%","25.10%"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Chapter 13: Financial Statement Analysis"}},{"itemId":"20a68d5b-8da9-549d-bce5-53a4075c235b","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"bd1915a5-24f2-40e1-9937-4ddf679dc264","sourceKey":"quiz:104","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"YCT LTD: PBIT N980,000; Capital Employed (SHF + LT liabilities) N4,592,500. What is the Return on Capital Employed?","options":["16.50%","18.90%","21.34%","24.60%"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Chapter 13: Financial Statement Analysis"}},{"itemId":"da124160-cc74-5a2b-8e4d-bf8f6d3e3c1e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d98442e6-f44a-47d6-9f76-038d44d8d0cd","sourceKey":"quiz:105","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Given: Stock N3,000; Cash N7,600; Average Debtor N10,000; Trade Creditors N2,900 (N'000). What is the Current Ratio?","options":["5.85","6.40","7.10","7.85"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Chapter 13: Financial Statement Analysis"}},{"itemId":"e5030339-d81f-5bf6-ad59-7afd2228b477","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f57fe7e9-9e02-4173-96d1-469ca59de6fd","sourceKey":"quiz:106","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Given: Net Profit before Tax N6,000; Interest on Loans N8,000 (N'000). What is the Fixed Interest Cover?","options":["1.35 times","1.55 times","1.75 times","1.95 times"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Chapter 13: Financial Statement Analysis"}},{"itemId":"097d4977-229b-56e2-add7-b37e1b8d8e1b","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"663a2ea6-5899-4ff4-a27d-c5b6ea4e4b84","sourceKey":"quiz:107","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Given: Stock N3,000; Cash N7,600; Average Debtor N10,000; Trade Creditors N2,900 (N'000). What is the Quick (Acid Test) Ratio?","options":["5.07","6.07","7.07","8.07"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Chapter 13: Financial Statement Analysis"}},{"itemId":"31eabaf8-defc-544b-817b-3932d8614602","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"22d5e0a4-3e9f-4d50-9f2a-326879ceee55","sourceKey":"quiz:108","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Given: Credit Sales N40,000; Average Debtor N10,000 (N'000). What is the Accounts Receivable Turnover?","options":["3 times","4 times","5 times","6 times"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Chapter 13: Financial Statement Analysis"}},{"itemId":"5bbed4d6-b262-5b69-b9b0-529bde982462","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3e0e378c-346a-4070-bb5d-7554c09105e0","sourceKey":"quiz:109","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Given: Net Profit before Tax N6,000 (N'000); Tax rate 35%; Shareholder Funds N160,000 (N'000). What is the Return on Shareholders' Equity (ROSE)?","options":["1.95%","2.44%","2.95%","3.40%"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Chapter 13: Financial Statement Analysis"}},{"itemId":"6e96673f-8c17-5fa5-8c7a-003d7146590b","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1d54bec1-6660-42c1-b213-c343f8557d52","sourceKey":"quiz:11","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"What interest rate compounded quarterly will make N5,000 accumulate to N20,000 in 4 years?","options":["24.15%","30.00%","33.75%","36.20%"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Chapter 2: Mathematics of Finance"}},{"itemId":"6624f5a2-1bec-505a-a3e6-dda5e288e66c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6af77230-7128-418a-a963-8e028b0fe003","sourceKey":"quiz:110","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Given: Net Profit before Tax N6,000; Interest N8,000 (giving PBIT N14,000); Average Total Assets N54,000 (N'000). What is the Return on Total Assets (ROTA)?","options":["18.75%","21.30%","25.93%","29.10%"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Chapter 13: Financial Statement Analysis"}},{"itemId":"86760142-97a7-5c37-bfe0-8e57dfbdd284","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"dce88d4a-f8e9-42b3-bd05-64423ce7e14c","sourceKey":"quiz:111","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Given: Average Debtor N10,000; Credit Sales N40,000 (N'000). What is the number of Days Receivable Outstanding?","options":["71.25 days","81.25 days","91.25 days","101.25 days"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Chapter 13: Financial Statement Analysis"}},{"itemId":"d3bee67a-7657-5adb-8487-b3fb3f313426","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"fea1d73e-ca1c-40c5-98bf-6eefc7880b61","sourceKey":"quiz:12","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"N100,000 is deposited at a bank offering 24% per annum payable twice a quarter (i.e., 8 times a year). What is the compounded value after 3 years?","options":["N185,943.00","N197,431.00","N203,279.41","N210,558.00"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Chapter 2: Mathematics of Finance"}},{"itemId":"05b609af-63ce-57ed-8d28-3a1ec6be6e91","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"31f680ea-4a2e-4198-8d1e-2e85d22cc102","sourceKey":"quiz:13","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A baby bond of N100,000 pays 6% per year compounded annually. What is it worth on the child's 21st birthday?","options":["N260,904.00","N300,000.00","N339,956.36","N378,160.00"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Chapter 2: Mathematics of Finance"}},{"itemId":"3078580f-b2d0-55b8-80ba-208d7df33619","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"dff321b2-fe45-47aa-8dec-0f8d60c4bfc8","sourceKey":"quiz:14","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"N250,000 is invested at 12.5% per annum, compounded on a half-year basis. What is the future value after 6 years?","options":["N450,300.00","N489,215.00","N517,472.50","N545,900.00"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Chapter 2: Mathematics of Finance"}},{"itemId":"54e023a0-0742-5f56-9837-122cfec859f9","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4ff3c408-3aac-4ad3-b494-61f27033d5b4","sourceKey":"quiz:15","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"What is the present value of N300,000 receivable in 5 years, at a yield of 15% per annum?","options":["N135,900.00","N142,600.00","N149,153.02","N156,800.00"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Chapter 2: Mathematics of Finance"}},{"itemId":"458c961d-cf21-5963-8180-5aa8a0762383","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e5d4b50d-9570-44ca-b15d-423704bc5aa0","sourceKey":"quiz:16","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"David inherited N400,000. Option X: First Bank at 10% p.a., interest paid monthly. Option Y: Zenith Bank at 15% p.a., interest paid quarterly. Option Z: Standard Trust Bank at 16% p.a., interest paid semi-annually. Which investment yields the highest return after 6 months?","options":["Option X – N420,421.33","Option Y – N430,562.50","Option Z – N432,000.00","All three are equal after 6 months"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Chapter 2: Mathematics of Finance"}},{"itemId":"9331422a-c486-5c83-9e91-70f73b7217e8","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4c4e6d2f-829a-4307-bdac-302282f2e69f","sourceKey":"quiz:17","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Find the future value: PV = N2,250, n = 30 years, rate = 17%.","options":["N198,430.00","N224,655.00","N249,895.46","N267,120.00"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Chapter 2: Mathematics of Finance"}},{"itemId":"1b350c0c-0649-57ad-b317-f32008b95d3e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e851c4b9-41ef-4f88-919b-51aaaa8e76e8","sourceKey":"quiz:18","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Find the future value: PV = N9,310, n = 15 years, rate = 6%.","options":["N18,905.00","N20,675.00","N22,311.96","N24,150.00"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Chapter 2: Mathematics of Finance"}},{"itemId":"c434b789-ac4d-594a-a7c3-3f139120d3ed","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b2d35d69-adc3-499c-9644-97d08a96fc8a","sourceKey":"quiz:19","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Find the future value: PV = N76,355, n = 2 years, rate = 12%.","options":["N85,340.00","N90,215.00","N95,779.71","N101,500.00"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Chapter 2: Mathematics of Finance"}},{"itemId":"83e7c102-3b33-5cce-b911-44b447b36f49","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b9e8b566-9e83-4918-8e75-8a587d8bf890","sourceKey":"quiz:2","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"John Nwachukwu can take N240,000 now or N360,000 in five years. He can earn 25% annually. Which option is better?","options":["Take N240,000 now (grows to ~N732,421.88 at 25%, far exceeding N360,000)","Take the deferred N360,000","Both are equal in value","Insufficient information to decide"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Chapter 2: Mathematics of Finance"}},{"itemId":"3e5ca068-f570-58d6-b2cc-04fee5bd5224","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"56c88f85-4ff5-4c89-913c-ae39f28779e6","sourceKey":"quiz:20","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Find the future value: PV = N183,790, n = 7 years, rate = 9%.","options":["N295,600.00","N312,450.00","N335,975.31","N358,900.00"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Chapter 2: Mathematics of Finance"}},{"itemId":"625581c6-ce7c-51ae-a7e0-0e25befc5bae","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"343cb7cb-c9ca-4e62-9c0b-4bd6659bf5c0","sourceKey":"quiz:21","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Dr. James needs N800,000 in 5 years. He will contribute an equal amount monthly, starting now, into a plan earning 36% compounded monthly. How much should he contribute each month?","options":["N3,890.20","N4,215.75","N4,763.46","N5,120.90"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Chapter 2: Mathematics of Finance","explanation":"Treated as an annuity due, since contributions start immediately."}},{"itemId":"48c68177-1e03-5ecd-bc46-cd55a32a3f74","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"fc9e84f4-2418-4b44-9b47-003de9d50db3","sourceKey":"quiz:22","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Mr. Adeniji has a N5.5m five-year mortgage at 24% per annum. He repays N3.48m at the end of year 1, with the balance in equal instalments over the remaining 4 years. What is the amount of each subsequent instalment?","options":["N1,180,450.00","N1,285,600.00","N1,389,191.20","N1,475,300.00"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Chapter 2: Mathematics of Finance","explanation":"Balance after year 1 = N5.5m × 1.24 − N3.48m = N3.34m, then amortised over 4 years at 24%."}},{"itemId":"69fbc376-269b-5d87-9cfc-9ad9160353a8","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"20d83c95-12f8-4449-a46d-c41aa168c485","sourceKey":"quiz:23","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"What annual premium will allow Mr. Fatukosi to claim N85,000 at the maturity of a 10-year endowment policy, at 18% per annum?","options":["N2,980.50","N3,340.20","N3,613.74","N3,950.00"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Chapter 2: Mathematics of Finance"}},{"itemId":"fffcb85e-f169-58d3-a64c-7376cce1d0ae","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f46565a0-0ede-4449-9146-8861761c0620","sourceKey":"quiz:24","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Mr. Andrew Clark bought a building for N200,000, made a N90,000 down payment, and financed the N110,000 balance over 5 years at 30% fixed interest. How much must he pay yearly?","options":["N38,900.00","N42,150.00","N45,163.97","N48,700.00"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Chapter 2: Mathematics of Finance"}},{"itemId":"fb22a93d-fc78-562b-9463-e44038568203","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"92c279d2-ec31-4cd1-8ecf-0e32a7055597","sourceKey":"quiz:25","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A driver offers to settle a N250-per-month claim over 20 years with a single lump sum today. To maximise your neighbour's settlement, which discount rate should you recommend, and what is the resulting lump sum?","options":["12% per annum – N22,704.85","12% per annum – N34,895.19","6% per annum – N22,704.85","6% per annum – N34,895.19"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Chapter 2: Mathematics of Finance","explanation":"A lower discount rate produces a higher present value, which benefits the party receiving the lump sum."}},{"itemId":"c53dc0b6-d317-5c2b-b57a-8e63d18bdaad","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f1d44fe6-79a4-40a6-8fa3-17cf9c9601bd","sourceKey":"quiz:26","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Dalaxy Property: Option 1 offers N600,000 now plus N150,000 quarterly for 10 years. Option 2 offers N1,740,000 in year 2, N2,100,000 in year 6, plus N240,000 quarterly for 3 years starting year 7. The applicable cost of funds is 21% per annum. Which option has the higher present value?","options":["Option 1 – PV ≈ N3,088,124.67","Option 2 – PV ≈ N3,088,124.67","Option 1 – PV ≈ N2,525,897.13","Both options have equal present value"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Chapter 2: Mathematics of Finance","explanation":"Option 2 PV ≈ N2,525,897.13, so Option 1 is preferable at a 21% cost of funds."}},{"itemId":"589ea4fc-13d8-5939-bf49-edd12aa0bf07","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"388a1fc7-2566-4f08-86e4-618b015e1437","sourceKey":"quiz:27","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Peter deposits N10,000 at the beginning of each quarter into an account yielding 16% per annum for the first 3 years, then 18% per annum for a further 2 years. What is the accumulated value at the end of year 5?","options":["N265,430.00","N298,610.00","N320,250.50","N345,900.00"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Chapter 2: Mathematics of Finance"}},{"itemId":"59c36f84-b5fc-553a-9db9-f62beca4aeaf","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d7c37b47-ee9c-488f-833d-2ea2d91ca243","sourceKey":"quiz:28","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Jegede's life expectancy is 15 years. First Continental Insurance uses 11% in its calculations. What must Jegede pay now for an annuity providing N50,000 per year (ordinary annuity, paid at year-end)?","options":["N298,650.00","N325,910.00","N359,543.48","N390,200.00"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Chapter 2: Mathematics of Finance"}},{"itemId":"3093a7b8-8fce-50ee-ba60-4e3c44157692","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"942ed16f-a89f-454c-bdc7-9bae598b7dc5","sourceKey":"quiz:29","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Equipment worth N1.6 million will be replaced in 4 years. Money can be invested at 15% compounded annually. How much must be deposited each year (ordinary annuity, sinking fund) to have the amount available?","options":["N275,900.00","N298,340.00","N320,424.56","N345,780.00"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Chapter 2: Mathematics of Finance"}},{"itemId":"b5ce48b6-afb0-5d1a-8e4b-2b750f4cdb04","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c37474a3-183b-4143-8560-2f6d9cdfd8a4","sourceKey":"quiz:3","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"How many years will it take N3,500 to accumulate to N45,000 at 25% compounded annually?","options":["9.4 years","10.2 years","11.4 years","13.1 years"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Chapter 2: Mathematics of Finance"}},{"itemId":"2396c6e1-22fc-53ba-ae66-dfb293f6582e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"dcde52ec-e029-4dda-83a3-7d04253fdbc0","sourceKey":"quiz:30","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Mrs. Parkes wants to provide her two sons N15,000 each (N30,000 total) per year for 5 years, first payment 1 year from now, at 17% compounded annually. How much should she invest now?","options":["N82,450.00","N89,600.00","N95,980.38","N101,750.00"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Chapter 2: Mathematics of Finance"}},{"itemId":"f3018274-d46a-58c6-9ab1-65777b47a65c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"32d80a1b-8b10-457d-9fcd-74466a74fb8d","sourceKey":"quiz:31","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Home State Housing offers a building for N1.2 million. Adamu pays N400,000 down, with the N800,000 balance payable in equal instalments over 4 years at 13% compounded annually. What is the annual payment?","options":["N232,600.00","N250,415.00","N268,955.36","N285,700.00"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Chapter 2: Mathematics of Finance"}},{"itemId":"f903d318-051d-5fe5-b5c3-847b83b9ffa3","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9bbc294f-c797-4489-800f-4437dc8b96e0","sourceKey":"quiz:32","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Mr. Kedeso needs N0.5m in 5 years. He will set aside an equal amount annually, invested immediately (annuity due), at 20% compounded interest. What amount should he set aside each year?","options":["N49,850.00","N55,991.54","N61,320.00","N67,189.85"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Chapter 2: Mathematics of Finance","explanation":"Because the deposit is invested immediately – an annuity due – the ordinary sinking-fund payment of N67,189.85 is discounted one period to N55,991.54."}},{"itemId":"d323cb7a-3048-5416-8e54-3d8a37b1308c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ba0e694a-cde5-4fbe-84eb-7e83851186fb","sourceKey":"quiz:33","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"An MBA student pays no fees now but pays 2% monthly interest and an equal monthly amount from salary. The MBA bill is N175,000 over 2 years (24 months). What is the constant monthly amount?","options":["N7, 850.00","N8,540.00","N9,252.44","N9,980.00"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Chapter 2: Mathematics of Finance"}},{"itemId":"0cc00cd7-42b3-5b54-9e57-cb8e95af2329","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ccc5df13-1fa1-4d2d-8fc4-00954544779e","sourceKey":"quiz:34","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Afolabi Helen invests N10,000 on April 1st of each year from 1983 to 1998 inclusive (16 payments, annuity due) at 13% per year. How much will she have accumulated by April 1st, 2001 (3 years after the last deposit)?","options":["N527,390.60","N612,480.00","N698,750.00","N760,970.32"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Chapter 2: Mathematics of Finance"}},{"itemId":"147f0aae-2a99-5ce6-bfe2-63d3d0877a94","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"17afdb3d-7e55-45b8-b751-464509a6bd9e","sourceKey":"quiz:35","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Nojeem State took a N100 million loan and will liquidate it via 5 equal annual payments at 19% compounded annually. What is the amount of each annual payment?","options":["N26,450,000.00","N29,880,500.00","N32,705,016.66","N35,910,200.00"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Chapter 2: Mathematics of Finance","explanation":"Interest in payment 1 = N19,000,000; balance after payment 1 = N86,294,983.34; interest in payment 2 = N16,396,046.84; balance after payment 2 = N69,986,013.52."}},{"itemId":"f38f3986-cb07-59b7-9ec8-494131e7226e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"395293d0-2b9b-43d1-b008-67fb57da4c1a","sourceKey":"quiz:36","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Sieku Limited's machine costs N7.5m today, with a 5-year effective life. Machine prices inflate at 14% per annum. What annual sinking fund deposit (accumulating at 25%) is needed to replace the machine?","options":["N1,485,300.00","N1,620,750.00","N1,759,541.17","N1,890,600.00"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Chapter 2: Mathematics of Finance","explanation":"Inflated replacement cost in 5 years ≈ N14,440,609.37."}},{"itemId":"c413ca7f-d59a-54bb-b754-27a908b2a882","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"acfa09bf-4c57-4e7b-b3fc-c2a7d072d822","sourceKey":"quiz:37","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Jegede deposits N50,000 at the beginning of every year (annuity due), earning 21% per annum, aiming for a N2,000,000 lump sum. In how many years will he reach this target?","options":["8 years","9 years","10 years","11 years"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Chapter 2: Mathematics of Finance","explanation":"Accumulated value after 11 years ≈ N2,057,079.21, the first year the target of N2,000,000 is met or exceeded."}},{"itemId":"d5339e73-25d6-539d-a387-dd1431c54b5e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"23c4710d-36e2-4b80-8827-247ad99d5c19","sourceKey":"quiz:38","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Blessing needs N500,000 in 6 years (72 months). How much must she save monthly, starting now, at 18% per annum, to meet this liability?","options":["N3,215.60","N3,560.90","N3,903.90","N4,250.30"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Chapter 2: Mathematics of Finance"}},{"itemId":"acf4ffa2-1ad0-52a8-af6b-c8ef97c1f904","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"55ba3839-3e3f-4237-8b58-3e58ff6b525a","sourceKey":"quiz:39","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which statement correctly distinguishes risk from uncertainty in capital budgeting?","options":["Risk and uncertainty are the same thing and can be used interchangeably","Risk exists when possible future outcomes and their probabilities can be estimated; uncertainty exists when outcomes cannot be assigned meaningful probabilities","Risk applies only to short-term decisions, while uncertainty applies only to long-term decisions","Risk is subjective while uncertainty is always objective and measurable"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Chapter 5: Capital Budgeting with Risk and Uncertainty"}},{"itemId":"db7c0ebb-4247-566e-b771-1d92ddfc19bc","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e371a099-4584-436f-b4e9-6000be69d953","sourceKey":"quiz:4","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Find the present value of N7,500 to be paid in 5 years, with interest compounded continuously at 20%.","options":["N2,759.10","N3,010.75","N2,500.00","N3,750.00"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Chapter 2: Mathematics of Finance"}},{"itemId":"26aa91a9-6a0d-5fc8-b0e1-36e80c5c3651","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0be3d672-feea-486f-8a6c-fba219104dea","sourceKey":"quiz:40","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT a standard method of treating risk in capital budgeting?","options":["Risk-adjusted discount rate","Certainty equivalent method","Sensitivity analysis","First-In-First-Out (FIFO) valuation"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Chapter 5: Capital Budgeting with Risk and Uncertainty","explanation":"FIFO is an inventory valuation method, not a capital budgeting risk-treatment technique (which include risk-adjusted discount rate, certainty equivalents, sensitivity analysis, probability analysis, decision trees, and simulation)."}},{"itemId":"08a8900a-89fa-59c0-b592-27e3fa3c4e5c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"238ebe5b-b6a0-41ec-b08d-b410302af0bd","sourceKey":"quiz:41","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"James is choosing between Securities A, B and C. Security A: N4,000 (P=0.4), N5,000 (P=0.5), N6,000 (P=0.1). Security B: N6,000 (P=0.7), N4,000 (P=0.2), N3,000 (P=0.1). Security C: N5,000 (P=0.1), N6,000 (P=0.3), N7,000 (P=0.6). Which security has the highest expected return?","options":["Security A – N4,700","Security B – N5,300","Security C – N6,500","All three are equal"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Chapter 5: Capital Budgeting with Risk and Uncertainty"}},{"itemId":"4b5fb12d-c5b5-5ca4-b688-90259d6468a9","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"46abfaa5-e080-4920-91df-148b4538721d","sourceKey":"quiz:42","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Using the coefficient of variation (CV = SD ÷ Expected Return) as the risk measure, which security should be recommended to James, a risk-averse investor?","options":["Security A (CV ≈ 0.136)","Security B (CV ≈ 0.208)","Security C (CV ≈ 0.103)","James should be indifferent between all three"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Chapter 5: Capital Budgeting with Risk and Uncertainty","explanation":"Security C has both the highest expected return (N6,500) and the lowest relative risk (CV ≈ 10.3%), making it the clear choice for a risk-averse investor."}},{"itemId":"03edd022-5ce2-5bdb-afeb-a1662ecd7420","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c4932597-7627-437c-80cb-6b5eb3d0d2f0","sourceKey":"quiz:43","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Adebisi Company is choosing among Projects X, Y and Z. Project X: N15,000(0.1), N22,000(0.3), N27,000(0.2), N30,000(0.4). Project Y: N20,000(0.4), N25,000(0.2), N30,000(0.3), N35,000(0.1). Project Z: N27,000(0.5), N30,000(0.2), N35,000(0.1), N40,000(0.2). Which project has the highest expected (mean) cash flow?","options":["Project X – N25,500","Project Y – N25,500","Project Z – N31,000","All three are equal"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Chapter 5: Capital Budgeting with Risk and Uncertainty"}},{"itemId":"2c42a219-a8b5-5f07-8cd1-8fd73b70d41d","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9b2de534-ea5f-4dab-b83b-85fb1fde318f","sourceKey":"quiz:44","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Using the coefficient of variation, which project is LESS risky?","options":["Project X (CV ≈ 0.189)","Project Y (CV ≈ 0.205)","Project Z (CV ≈ 0.164)","All three carry identical risk"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Chapter 5: Capital Budgeting with Risk and Uncertainty","explanation":"Project Z has both the highest expected cash flow and the lowest coefficient of variation, making it the least risky per unit of return among the three."}},{"itemId":"2a9ba479-30aa-514f-a84f-c03c1f876ab2","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8e744c52-2a5e-4d63-92f7-50a1eaef0b6a","sourceKey":"quiz:45","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following best defines the \"working capital cycle\"?","options":["The time between paying for raw materials and receiving cash from customers for the goods sold","The total value of a firm's fixed assets","The ratio of long-term debt to shareholders' funds","The time it takes a firm to prepare its annual financial statements"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Chapter 10: Working Capital Management"}},{"itemId":"c8ea64e1-be83-5b4c-932c-ac9e9be260c4","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c146f106-570b-4917-8563-5c0297357847","sourceKey":"quiz:46","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is one of the basic steps involved in cash management?","options":["Determining the optimal cash conversion/collection cycle and accelerating cash inflows","Maximising the depreciation charge on fixed assets","Minimising the number of ordinary shares in issue","Eliminating all current liabilities from the balance sheet"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Chapter 10: Working Capital Management","explanation":"The basic steps typically outlined are: (1) forecasting cash flows, (2) managing/accelerating collections, (3) controlling disbursements, and (4) investing surplus cash."}},{"itemId":"3de96f19-8656-56e6-9a0a-53445d46a2f8","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"450a505e-c97c-4aca-b661-19433c180a4a","sourceKey":"quiz:47","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is a determinant of a firm's working capital requirement?","options":["The nature and size of the business","The par value of ordinary shares","The location of the company's head office","The name of the company's external auditor"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Chapter 10: Working Capital Management","explanation":"Other determinants include the length of the operating/cash cycle, sales growth, seasonality, credit policy, and production cycle."}},{"itemId":"beb9c571-6e86-50d7-b3bf-db97b920b034","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f08b1e70-4d33-43b2-8e12-7e331bccf5ef","sourceKey":"quiz:48","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"What is the % cost of NOT taking a cash discount of 5% for settlement within 5 days, with a maximum credit period of 30 days (360-day year)?","options":["45.26%","60.00%","75.79%","90.15%"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Chapter 10: Working Capital Management","explanation":"Cost = [d/(100−d)] × [360/(net period − discount period)] = (5/95) × (360/25) = 75.79%."}},{"itemId":"ea8eef8f-c729-5b69-b432-7453f2dc0e75","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"60a2e9d0-8a36-499d-8239-ea306a1ebc66","sourceKey":"quiz:49","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"What is the % cost of NOT taking a cash discount of 2% for settlement within 10 days, with a maximum credit period of 60 days (360-day year)?","options":["9.18%","14.69%","18.20%","22.50%"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Chapter 10: Working Capital Management","explanation":"Cost = (2/98) × (360/50) = 14.69%."}},{"itemId":"517305f5-7be5-58a2-a9f4-6a0d3c700a05","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"81abae23-6864-4915-93bc-2ef2be3db9b4","sourceKey":"quiz:5","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"N750 is compounded continuously at 13½% for 8 years. What is the value at the end of 8 years?","options":["N1,980.00","N2,208.51","N2,450.75","N2,100.30"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Chapter 2: Mathematics of Finance","explanation":"Effective annual rate under continuous compounding ≈ 14.45%; under semi-annual compounding at the nominal 13.5%, the effective annual rate would be ≈ 13.96%."}},{"itemId":"13954f70-6ea7-5def-849f-11ed827a86c8","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"05be43e2-fca5-4dab-b670-eeb8444e82c5","sourceKey":"quiz:50","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"If credit terms are 2/10 net 45, what is the % cost of not taking the cash discount (360-day year)?","options":["14.69%","18.32%","20.99%","24.10%"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Chapter 10: Working Capital Management","explanation":"Cost = (2/98) × (360/35) = 20.99%."}},{"itemId":"2c881d6a-f3dc-5df9-bc38-b003fd7248e6","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"86eac208-303b-4330-bb9d-673601fe38bf","sourceKey":"quiz:51","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"A firm currently sells on \"30 days net\" and is considering switching to \"3/10 net 30\" terms. What is the % cost of the new credit policy (360-day year)?","options":["42.10%","48.35%","55.67%","61.20%"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Chapter 10: Working Capital Management","explanation":"Cost = (3/97) × (360/20) = 55.67%."}},{"itemId":"df51a4f0-ee3c-54b5-a3c9-17612dc5d2af","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4bed8c5b-d8a5-4c92-a887-7fe515915496","sourceKey":"quiz:52","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following best describes the Economic Order Quantity (EOQ)?","options":["The order quantity that minimises the total of ordering costs and carrying (holding) costs","The maximum quantity a supplier is willing to sell in a single order","The order quantity that maximises total purchase cost","The quantity of stock a firm holds as a safety buffer against stock-outs"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Chapter 10: Working Capital Management","explanation":"EOQ assumes constant demand, known ordering and carrying costs, no stock-outs, and instantaneous replenishment. EOQ = √(2DS/H), where D = annual demand, S = cost per order, H = carrying cost per unit."}},{"itemId":"7eeb3f83-d7a1-5e63-a9d8-ce97679b1b5a","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"30a03b2c-1e50-4c1d-93ff-9ea19b9f03c8","sourceKey":"quiz:53","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Wazobia Plc uses 200,000 units of \"quine\" per year. Ordering cost is N15/order; annual carrying cost is N50/unit. What is the Economic Order Quantity (EOQ)?","options":["246 units","346 units","446 units","546 units"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Chapter 10: Working Capital Management","explanation":"EOQ = √(2 × 200,000 × 15 / 50) = √120,000 ≈ 346 units."}},{"itemId":"974c7425-afdc-50b3-88c4-5fc96676835c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4aeff5eb-04b8-42c4-8e33-987f7b60c389","sourceKey":"quiz:54","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Using the EOQ of 346 units, approximately how many orders will Wazobia Plc place per year?","options":["377 orders","477 orders","577 orders","677 orders"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Chapter 10: Working Capital Management","explanation":"Orders per year = 200,000/346 ≈ 577."}},{"itemId":"501a58dc-0277-5836-8b79-e3259902fb13","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7aa37c7e-dda4-4618-a75f-1240a2ddf0f2","sourceKey":"quiz:55","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"What is the total ordering cost per year at the EOQ?","options":["N6,660.25","N7,660.25","N8,660.25","N9,660.25"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Chapter 10: Working Capital Management"}},{"itemId":"f3a5fb75-3cb6-5ea5-b2ca-cdbe8499b23f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"18b2df63-8867-4230-987d-a04d2a253071","sourceKey":"quiz:56","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"What is the total carrying (holding) cost per year at the EOQ?","options":["N6,660.25","N7,660.25","N8,660.25","N9,660.25"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Chapter 10: Working Capital Management","explanation":"At the EOQ, total ordering cost always equals total carrying cost — both are N8,660.25."}},{"itemId":"9d4733e9-ca42-596f-8bdf-825df3d136f0","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ff5a3e4a-69bf-4c07-9abf-fd4a62ef860e","sourceKey":"quiz:57","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"What is the total purchase cost of the 200,000 units per year, at N180/unit?","options":["N30,000,000","N33,000,000","N36,000,000","N39,000,000"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Chapter 10: Working Capital Management"}},{"itemId":"6d623126-c40f-5fd5-9709-ca7b6985c35d","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7444d596-c164-4986-ad7d-3f19ef8c457d","sourceKey":"quiz:58","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"What is the total material cost that CAN be controlled for the year (ordering cost + carrying cost)?","options":["N15,320.51","N17,320.51","N19,320.51","N21,320.51"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Chapter 10: Working Capital Management"}},{"itemId":"2fab9ed0-9a73-570a-bff4-c9afae7a7827","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9fc3f2fe-13c1-4880-82cf-b114a2aa63f1","sourceKey":"quiz:59","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"What is the total material cost that CANNOT be controlled for the year (the purchase cost, which does not vary with order size)?","options":["N30,000,000.00","N33,000,000.00","N36,000,000.00","N39,000,000.00"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Chapter 10: Working Capital Management"}},{"itemId":"01cf4720-d6f8-5b6f-af86-c549da8486ae","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"63ed1c39-94b9-439c-9064-09f11b241daf","sourceKey":"quiz:6","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"How long will it take N5,000 to accumulate to N30,000 at 20% per annum?","options":["7.5 years","8.6 years","9.8 years","11.2 years"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Chapter 2: Mathematics of Finance"}},{"itemId":"b93b1a5c-789d-56b5-ade5-774e064ec47d","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"19feaf4d-d229-43b8-b4b0-9d58420517af","sourceKey":"quiz:60","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following best defines \"financial ratio analysis\"?","options":["The process of expressing relationships between figures in financial statements to assess a firm's performance and position","The process of converting foreign currency financial statements into naira","The process of auditing a company's financial statements for fraud","The process of preparing a company's tax returns"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Chapter 13: Financial Statement Analysis"}},{"itemId":"c3212acf-5e82-58ed-a5be-4da1c123d43e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"204abaf5-7eb6-4a4f-81aa-4f5c55396255","sourceKey":"quiz:61","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Financial ratios are commonly grouped into which of the following broad categories?","options":["Liquidity, profitability, gearing/solvency, efficiency/activity, and investor/stock market ratios","Only liquidity and profitability ratios","Only ratios required by the Companies and Allied Matters Act","Marketing ratios and human resource ratios"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Chapter 13: Financial Statement Analysis"}},{"itemId":"0a873601-26e8-5af3-b91a-6cc3c756c1cc","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ee7a3d28-ba9d-4f86-baa1-ace4eae01d0e","sourceKey":"quiz:62","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"What is the correct formula for the Price Earning (P/E) Ratio?","options":["Market Price per Share ÷ Earning per Share","Earning per Share ÷ Market Price per Share","Market Price per Share × Number of Shares","Dividend per Share ÷ Market Price per Share"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Chapter 13: Financial Statement Analysis"}},{"itemId":"6a3548f2-3a80-5fc5-967d-e3372efbd3a6","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"13a93828-7770-447c-adf0-953036d97554","sourceKey":"quiz:63","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"What is the correct formula for Earning Per Share (EPS)?","options":["Total Assets ÷ Number of Shares","(Earnings After Interest and Tax less Preference Dividend) ÷ Issued Ordinary Shares ranking for Dividend","Net Sales ÷ Number of Shares","Market Price per Share ÷ Dividend per Share"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Chapter 13: Financial Statement Analysis"}},{"itemId":"ca375025-8276-5914-ac16-b16be518e2fc","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d208c195-088c-4e9d-b0a1-f4172609be0a","sourceKey":"quiz:64","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"What is the correct formula for the Current Ratio?","options":["Current Assets ÷ Current Liabilities","(Current Assets − Stock) ÷ Current Liabilities","Total Assets ÷ Total Liabilities","Fixed Assets ÷ Current Liabilities"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Chapter 13: Financial Statement Analysis"}},{"itemId":"49ce026b-a8e4-595d-a28d-080e960f57ab","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5af1c7ae-9a3e-44b5-8a5a-df75d200b2d0","sourceKey":"quiz:65","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"What is the correct formula for the Acid Test (Quick) Ratio?","options":["Current Assets ÷ Current Liabilities","(Current Assets − Stock) ÷ Current Liabilities","Cash ÷ Current Liabilities","(Current Assets − Debtors) ÷ Current Liabilities"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Chapter 13: Financial Statement Analysis"}},{"itemId":"3955cc3e-b28d-523a-9090-a9965f7b69ed","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c6de79b3-7fe3-4d5d-921a-518359232f52","sourceKey":"quiz:66","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"What is the correct formula for Dividend Yield?","options":["(Dividend per Share ÷ Market Price per Share) × 100","(Dividend per Share ÷ Earning per Share) × 100","(Earning per Share ÷ Market Price per Share) × 100","(Total Dividend ÷ Total Assets) × 100"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Chapter 13: Financial Statement Analysis"}},{"itemId":"cb2ca45b-030a-557d-bef6-ed0ff6afe08d","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"467187b9-389f-42f8-882a-3c80a45596c2","sourceKey":"quiz:67","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"What is the correct formula for the Debtors (Average Collection Period) Ratio?","options":["(Debtors ÷ Credit Sales) × 365","(Credit Sales ÷ Debtors) × 365","(Debtors ÷ Total Assets) × 365","(Creditors ÷ Purchases) × 365"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Chapter 13: Financial Statement Analysis"}},{"itemId":"46d7a036-ff85-5544-a07f-aeb3bc4c77bb","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f69c0bbd-88fc-4bc0-8896-72028f76a0b8","sourceKey":"quiz:68","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"\"To be useful, a calculated ratio needs to be compared with other ratios.\" Which of the following is NOT one of the standard types of comparison used?","options":["Trend (time-series) comparison — the same firm's ratios over several periods","Cross-sectional (inter-firm) comparison — against competitors or industry averages","Comparison against budgeted/planned targets","Comparison against the ratios of an unrelated company in a different industry"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Chapter 13: Financial Statement Analysis","explanation":"The three standard comparisons are: trend analysis, inter-firm/industry comparison, and comparison against budget or target ratios. Comparing against an unrelated firm in a different industry is not meaningful."}},{"itemId":"8fc26a4f-596c-530d-9ca4-ccf85ec1135f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"90c30f1a-2c2b-4f5a-8095-ca73dccf7a17","sourceKey":"quiz:69","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which group is NOT typically considered a primary user of financial ratio analysis?","options":["Investors and shareholders","Creditors and lenders","Management","The general public with no financial interest in the firm"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Chapter 13: Financial Statement Analysis","explanation":"Key users include management, investors, creditors/lenders, employees, government/tax authorities, and potential investors/acquirers."}},{"itemId":"bedb024e-30d3-5e60-a5a1-bb823af78cd7","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"71ad869e-492a-4fbb-8a49-8a0cc56ae86c","sourceKey":"quiz:7","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Joe Adams has N250,000 to invest for two years. Option (i): ABC Bank at 18½% per annum (simple annual compounding). Option (ii): Akoka Savings and Loans at 18½% compounded quarterly. Option (iii): Prudent Mortgage Finance at 18½% compounded continuously. Which option yields the highest return?","options":["ABC Bank – N351,056.25","Akoka Savings and Loans – N358,941.59","Prudent Mortgage Finance – N361,933.65","All three yield identical returns"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Chapter 2: Mathematics of Finance"}},{"itemId":"553f5084-aae7-5031-ab5c-d7c6bcadd642","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ca1fceee-e894-49e9-bbff-d936279ddc21","sourceKey":"quiz:70","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Why should users of financial ratios be cautious?","options":["Ratios are based on historical accounting data and can be affected by different accounting policies, window-dressing, and inflation","Ratios are always 100% accurate predictors of future performance","Ratios cannot be manipulated by management","Ratio analysis eliminates the need for any further investigation"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Chapter 13: Financial Statement Analysis"}},{"itemId":"e5d5bc76-a915-5780-9175-911e36c95f98","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"87b8de7d-6b23-48b3-8137-3203486a1413","sourceKey":"quiz:71","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is a key reason firms prepare financial statements?","options":["To provide information useful to shareholders, creditors, and other stakeholders for decision-making","To increase the physical size of the company's fixed assets","To avoid ever paying company income tax","Financial statements are optional and rarely prepared in practice"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Chapter 13: Financial Statement Analysis"}},{"itemId":"b418cfbb-93f7-5a0b-9b76-dd1b393b10d7","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"bff637c0-03fe-49f9-909f-89e7aedbae6c","sourceKey":"quiz:72","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is a recognised LIMITATION (not a merit) of financial ratio analysis?","options":["Ratios are based on historical data and may not reflect current or future conditions","Ratios allow quick, simplified comparison of performance over time","Ratios help identify trends in a firm's financial position","Ratios are useful for comparing firms of different sizes"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Chapter 13: Financial Statement Analysis"}},{"itemId":"58b75a16-cae6-5b70-bbab-de9d77e8a8ad","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e6009a9e-57f3-48ec-b327-693c8e732ad0","sourceKey":"quiz:73","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"OBEY NIG PLC (Dec 2003): Current Assets N17,360; Current Liabilities N6,250. What is the Current Ratio?","options":["2.28","2.58","2.78","3.08"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Chapter 13: Financial Statement Analysis"}},{"itemId":"2c2aca01-e8fa-529c-8020-4a27830ac860","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"02fbd253-e482-4bc6-96b8-354edbbb3ec6","sourceKey":"quiz:74","parentItemId":null,"revision":1,"payload":{"prompt":"OBEY NIG PLC: Current Assets N17,360; Inventories N3,100; Current Liabilities N6,250. What is the Quick (Acid Test) Ratio?","options":["1.98","2.28","2.58","2.78"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Chapter 13